{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KySCsFSXLbKW7WHwiSsaRP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Claims scoped to ''Walker Walk + Cheetah Run with consistent\n2\t  mechanism''. Cartpole either dropped or presented as preliminary with borderline\n3\t  CIVO signal (nve 1.2-1.5x, below diagnostic threshold).\n4\t\n5\t  NVE 1.5x threshold presented with environment-specific calibration caveat rather\n6\t  than universal diagnostic. Cross-env accuracy ~80% (Walker 100%, Cheetah 67%, Cartpole\n7\t  50% at 1.5x; improves to ~90%+ at 1.3x).\n8\t\n9\t  Dual-pathway finding (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B) mentioned in discussion\n10\t  as ''evidence of deeper fragility'' but NOT elevated to core claim — Pathway B remains\n11\t  under-characterized (2/4 seeds, mechanism unclear).\n12\t\n13\t  C2 strength adjusted: ''reliably triggers'' → ''triggers with high probability in\n14\t  locomotion tasks''. Add explicit limitation on environment coverage.\n15\t\n16\t  TAWM contrast still holds (dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based\n17\t  AC). Still submittable to CoRL as diagnostic paper; reviewers likely request revisions\n18\t  on generality.\n19\t\n20\t  '\n21\timagined_experiments:\n22\t- name: Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\n23\t  plan_id: plan_011\n24\t  description: Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\n25\t  expected_results:\n26\t    seed1_009e_eval_105K: 131\n27\t    seed1_009e_nve_peak: 4.12\n28\t    seed1_009e_nve_peak_step: 55K\n29\t    seed1_009e_collapse: true\n30\t    seed1_009e_collapse_onset: 35K\n31\t    seed2_009b_eval_105K: 268\n32\t    seed2_009b_nve_peak: 2.05\n33\t    seed2_009b_nve_peak_step: 25K\n34\t    seed2_009b_collapse: false\n35\t    seed2_009b_self_correction: true\n36\t    seed2_009b_nve_105K: 0.38\n37\t    collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n38\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 2/2 (100%)\n39\t    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: 2/2\n40\t  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\n41\t\n42\t    Trained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic\n43\t    marker.\n44\t\n45\t    Collapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints\n46\t    (feedback loop locks in); seed 2''s overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K),\n47\t    allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\n48\t\n49\t    The bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay\n50\t    buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm\n51\t    corrective gradients.\n52\t\n53\t    Under median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized;\n54\t    these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\n55\t\n56\t    '\n57\t  tables:\n58\t  - title: 009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\n59\t    rows:\n60\t    - step: 25K\n61\t      s1_eval: 167\n62\t      s1_nve: 1.78\n63\t      s2_eval: 141\n64\t      s2_nve: 2.05\n65\t    - step: 35K\n66\t      s1_eval: 146\n67\t      s1_nve: 1.71\n68\t      s2_eval: 164\n69\t      s2_nve: 1.24\n70\t    - step: 45K\n71\t      s1_eval: 141\n72\t      s1_nve: 2.09\n73\t      s2_eval: 141\n74\t      s2_nve: 1.62\n75\t    - step: 55K\n76\t      s1_eval: 83\n77\t      s1_nve: 4.12\n78\t      s2_eval: 172\n79\t      s2_nve: 1.41\n80\t    - step: 65K\n81\t      s1_eval: 250\n82\t      s1_nve: 1.23\n83\t      s2_eval: 209\n84\t      s2_nve: 0.94\n85\t    - step: 85K\n86\t      s1_eval: 202\n87\t      s1_nve: 1.15\n88\t      s2_eval: 236\n89\t      s2_nve: 0.51\n90\t    - step: 105K\n91\t      s1_eval: 131\n92\t      s1_nve: 1.57\n93\t      s2_eval: 268\n94\t      s2_nve: 0.38\n95\t  figures_description:\n96\t  - Learning curves (eval return) for 008d seed 0 (original), 009e (seed 1), 009b\n97\t    (seed 2) overlaid. Collapse at 55K for seeds 0 & 1; rising trajectory for seed\n98\t    2.\n99\t  - NVE trajectory showing 1.5x threshold line. Both seeds cross threshold; seed 2\n100\t    crosses back down (self-correction), seed 1 sustains above.\n101\t  analysis: 'Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold\n102\t    = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\n103\t\n104\t    Combined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This\n105\t    is the paper''s strongest quantitative result for C2.\n106\t\n107\t    '\n108\t- name: Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\n109\t  plan_id: plan_012\n110\t  description: Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\n111\t  expected_results:\n112\t    seed1_009f_eval_105K: 470\n113\t    seed1_009f_nve_peak: 0.95\n114\t    seed1_009f_collapse: false\n115\t    seed1_009f_trend: zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\n116\t    seed2_009c_eval_105K: 148\n117\t    seed2_009c_nve_peak: 1.26\n118\t    seed2_009c_nve_105K: 1.14\n119\t    seed2_009c_collapse: false\n120\t    seed2_009c_trend: peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but\n121\t      persistent decline\n122\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 0/2 (0%)\n123\t    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained: 2/2\n124\t    cross_seed_consistency: poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\n125\t  result_rationale: '009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed;\n126\t    re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\n127\t\n128\t    Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic\n129\t    cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve\n130\t    stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\n131\t\n132\t    The cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward\n133\t    prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The\n134\t    reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d''s\n135\t    2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\n136\t\n137\t    C2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x\n138\t    (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward\n139\t    quality trade-off.\n140\t\n141\t    '\n142\t  tables:\n143\t  - title: 008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\n144\t    rows:\n145\t    - step: 25K\n146\t      s0_nve: 1.02\n147\t      s1_nve: 0.95\n148\t      s2_nve: 1.26\n149\t    - step: 45K\n150\t      s0_nve: 0.83\n151\t      s1_nve: 0.75\n152\t      s2_nve: 0.72\n153\t    - step: 65K\n154\t      s0_nve: 0.37\n155\t      s1_nve: 0.45\n156\t      s2_nve: 0.53\n157\t    - step: 85K\n158\t      s0_nve: 0.65\n159\t      s1_nve: 0.55\n160\t      s2_nve: 0.6\n161\t    - step: 105K\n162\t      s0_nve: 0.3\n163\t      s1_nve: 0.4\n164\t      s2_nve: 1.14\n165\t  figures_description:\n166\t  - NVE trajectories for 008f (3 seeds) all below 1.5x threshold line. Contrast with\n167\t    008d trained dt_emb (2/2 exceed 1.5x).\n168\t  analysis: 'All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification\n169\t    for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\n170\t\n171\t    009c''s late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent\n172\t    with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\n173\t\n174\t    The practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal\n175\t    control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\n176\t\n177\t    '\n178\t- name: Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\n179\t  plan_id: plan_013\n180\t  description: Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample\n181\t    OFF + H=15 config.\n182\t  expected_results:\n183\t    seed1_eval_105K: 720\n184\t    seed1_nve_range:\n185\t    - 0.55\n186\t    - 0.85\n187\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n188\t    seed1_collapse: false\n189\t    seed1_vs_baseline_105K: -21%\n190\t    seed1_zigzag_pattern: true\n191\t    seed2_eval_105K: 680\n192\t    seed2_nve_range:\n193\t    - 0.6\n194\t    - 0.9\n195\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.7\n196\t    seed2_collapse: false\n197\t    seed2_vs_baseline_105K: -26%\n198\t    seed2_zigzag_pattern: true\n199\t    mean_eval_105K: 726\n200\t    mean_vs_baseline: -21%\n201\t    nve_structural_suppression_confirmed: true\n202\t  result_rationale: '008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with\n203\t    two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the\n204\t    structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\n205\t\n206\t    The zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never\n207\t    exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still\n208\t    introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by\n209\t    self-correction from real environment data.\n210\t\n211\t    NVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature\n212\t    of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens\n213\t    over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline,\n214\t    confirming it''s a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\n215\t\n216\t    Seed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0''s 778\n217\t    (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align\n218\t    favorably.\n219\t\n220\t    '\n221\t  tables:\n222\t  - title: 008a multi-seed summary\n223\t    rows:\n224\t    - seed: 0\n225\t      eval_105K: 778\n226\t      nve_105K: 0.59\n227\t      vs_baseline: -15%\n228\t      zigzag_cycles: 2\n229\t    - seed: 1\n230\t      eval_105K: 720\n231\t      nve_105K: 0.62\n232\t      vs_baseline: -21%\n233\t      zigzag_cycles: 2\n234\t    - seed: 2\n235\t      eval_105K: 680\n236\t      nve_105K: 0.7\n237\t      vs_baseline: -26%\n238\t      zigzag_cycles: 1.5\n239\t  figures_description:\n240\t  - Three-seed overlay of 008a eval curves showing synchronous zigzag pattern. All\n241\t    seeds show dip-recovery cycles with no terminal collapse.\n242\t  - Three-seed NVE overlay showing consistent suppression below 1.0x from 55K onwards.\n243\t  analysis: 'Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse).\n244\t    C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\n245\t\n246\t    Performance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed\n247\t    variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\n248\t\n249\t    Key contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed\n250\t    1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\n251\t\n252\t    '\n253\t- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\n254\t  plan_id: plan_014\n255\t  description: Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests\n256\t    whether CIVO is eliminated.\n257\t  expected_results:\n258\t    seed1_eval_105K: 870\n259\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.8\n260\t    seed1_collapse: false\n261\t    seed1_pathway: healthy\n262\t    seed2_eval_105K: 137\n263\t    seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n264\t    seed2_collapse: true\n265\t    seed2_collapse_onset: 95K\n266\t    seed2_pathway: Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\n267\t    seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse: -3.83\n268\t    seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse: -5.21\n269\t    collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n270\t    c6_verdict: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\n271\t    gate_dt_1_frac: 1.0\n272\t  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\n273\t\n274\t    Seed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates\n275\t    CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value\n276\t    exploitation.\n277\t\n278\t    Seed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This\n279\t    collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\n280\t\n281\t    — 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits\n282\t    to wrong policy)\n283\t\n284\t    — 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\n285\t\n286\t    This is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions\n287\t    in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The\n288\t    dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\n289\t\n290\t    009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not\n291\t    seed-fragile.\n292\t\n293\t    Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse\n294\t    rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn''t reduce collapse\n295\t    probability — it changes the pathway.\n296\t\n297\t    '\n298\t  tables:\n299\t  - title: Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\n300\t    rows:\n301\t    - feature: value_change\n302\t      pathway_A_008d_s1: +130% (60→138)\n303\t      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: +13% (339→384)\n304\t    - feature: eval_change\n305\t      pathway_A_008d_s1: -43% (146→83)\n306\t      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: -81% (715→137)\n307\t    - feature: nve_driver\n308\t      pathway_A_008d_s1: value numerator (explosion)\n309\t      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: eval denominator (crash)\n310\t    - feature: actor_entropy\n311\t      pathway_A_008d_s1: ↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\n312\t      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: ↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\n313\t    - feature: mechanism\n314\t      pathway_A_008d_s1: critic overestimate → actor exploit\n315\t      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\n316\t  figures_description:\n317\t  - 'Side-by-side panels: (a) 008e_v3 seed 1 healthy learning curve vs seed 2 late-stage\n318\t    collapse at 95K. (b) NVE trajectories diverge sharply at 95K. (c) Actor entropy:\n319\t    seed 1 stable, seed 2 rises during collapse.'\n320\t  analysis: 'C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection.\n321\t    Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\n322\t\n323\t    Pathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different\n324\t    from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\n325\t\n326\t    Under median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate\n327\t    with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as\n328\t    ''evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding''.\n329\t\n330\t    '\n331\t- name: Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\n332\t  plan_id: plan_015\n333\t  description: 'D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init).\n334\t    Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.'\n335\t  expected_results:\n336\t    eval_105K: 204\n337\t    nve_105K: 0.42\n338\t    nve_range:\n339\t    - 0.39\n340\t    - 0.47\n341\t    collapse: false\n342\t    collapse_mechanism: gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\n343\t    actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n344\t    actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n345\t    gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n346\t    vs_baseline_105K: -78%\n347\t    verdict: NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\n348\t  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\n349\t\n350\t    Frozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic\n351\t    cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding.\n352\t    This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\n353\t\n354\t    However, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is\n355\t    random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero\n356\t    gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely\n357\t    degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\n358\t\n359\t    This result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides\n360\t    a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational\n361\t    capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical\n362\t    dt conditioning.\n363\t\n364\t    Contributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation}\n365\t    × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each\n366\t    intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\n367\t\n368\t    '\n369\t  tables: []\n370\t  figures_description:\n371\t  - Eval trajectory showing high volatility (80-238) with no convergence pattern,\n372\t    contrasted with 008d's directional collapse and 008a's zigzag recovery.\n373\t  analysis: 'Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not\n374\t    a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\n375\t\n376\t    Combined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but\n377\t    trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb\n378\t    on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\n379\t\n380\t    '\n381\t- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\n382\t  plan_id: plan_016\n383\t  description: Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\n384\t  expected_results:\n385\t    seed3_eval_105K: 830\n386\t    seed3_nve_105K: 0.72\n387\t    seed3_collapse: false\n388\t    seed3_pathway: healthy\n389\t    seed4_eval_105K: 195\n390\t    seed4_nve_105K: 2.85\n391\t    seed4_collapse: true\n392\t    seed4_collapse_onset: 85K\n393\t    seed4_pathway: Pathway_B (tentative)\n394\t    seed4_actor_entropy_trend: rising during collapse\n395\t    total_collapse_rate_4_seeds: 2/4 (50%)\n396\t    pathway_B_characterization: under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent\n397\t      across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\n398\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized.\n399\t    The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional\n400\t    seeds collapses.\n401\t\n402\t    Seed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly\n403\t    to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway\n404\t    A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn''t fire.\n405\t\n406\t    Seed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops\n407\t    first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due\n408\t    to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\n409\t\n410\t    2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish\n411\t    from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median\n412\t    tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core\n413\t    claim.\n414\t\n415\t    The under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but\n416\t    cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model\n417\t    representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift,\n418\t    or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\n419\t\n420\t    '\n421\t  tables:\n422\t  - title: 008e_v3 all-seed summary\n423\t    rows:\n424\t    - seed: 1\n425\t      eval_105K: 870\n426\t      nve_105K: 0.8\n427\t      collapsed: false\n428\t      pathway: healthy\n429\t    - seed: 2\n430\t      eval_105K: 137\n431\t      nve_105K: 3.78\n432\t      collapsed: true\n433\t      pathway: B\n434\t    - seed: 3\n435\t      eval_105K: 830\n436\t      nve_105K: 0.72\n437\t      collapsed: false\n438\t      pathway: healthy\n439\t    - seed: 4\n440\t      eval_105K: 195\n441\t      nve_105K: 2.85\n442\t      collapsed: true\n443\t      pathway: B\n444\t  figures_description:\n445\t  - 'Four-seed overlay: two healthy trajectories (seeds 1,3 converging ~830-870) and\n446\t    two late-stage collapses (seeds 2,4 at 95K and 85K respectively). Shaded region\n447\t    shows collapse zone.'\n448\t  analysis: '2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination.\n449\t    dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\n450\t\n451\t    Pathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern\n452\t    is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\n453\t\n454\t    Paper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as\n455\t    diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\n456\t\n457\t    '\n458\t- name: Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\n459\t  plan_id: plan_020\n460\t  description: dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion\n461\t    environment.\n462\t  expected_results:\n463\t    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n464\t      5K: 95\n465\t      15K: 230\n466\t      25K: 380\n467\t      35K: 490\n468\t      45K: 555\n469\t      55K: 610\n470\t      65K: 645\n471\t      75K: 670\n472\t      85K: 695\n473\t      95K: 710\n474\t      105K: 720\n475\t    seed0_eval_peak: 755\n476\t    seed0_eval_peak_step: 285K\n477\t    seed0_eval_last10_mean: 738\n478\t    seed1_eval_final: 720\n479\t    seed1_eval_peak: 733\n480\t    seed1_note: anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\n481\t    seed2_eval_final: 695\n482\t    seed2_eval_peak: 725\n483\t    iqm_final: 718\n484\t    iqm_last10: 722\n485\t    ve_ratio_stable: ~65-70% from 135K+\n486\t    ve_ratio_25K: 0.095\n487\t    ve_ratio_35K: 0.21\n488\t    ve_ratio_45K: 0.305\n489\t    ve_ratio_55K: 0.385\n490\t    ve_ratio_65K: 0.49\n491\t    ve_ratio_75K: 0.58\n492\t    ve_ratio_105K: 0.65\n493\t  result_rationale: 'Cheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3\n494\t    typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final\n495\t    eval is [650, 800].\n496\t\n497\t    exp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current\n498\t    657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence\n499\t    than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\n500\t\n501\t    Cheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality\n502\t    observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns\n503\t    Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker\n504\t    due to fast joint movements.\n505\t\n506\t    v/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic\n507\t    architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker''s\n508\t    69-72% due to Cheetah''s higher prediction difficulty.\n509\t\n510\t    '\n511\t  tables:\n512\t  - title: Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\n513\t    rows:\n514\t    - seed: 0\n515\t      eval_peak: 755\n516\t      eval_final_10_mean: 738\n517\t      ve_stable: 67%\n518\t    - seed: 1\n519\t      eval_peak: 733\n520\t      eval_final_10_mean: 720\n521\t      ve_stable: 65%\n522\t    - seed: 2\n523\t      eval_peak: 725\n524\t      eval_final_10_mean: 695\n525\t      ve_stable: 66%\n526\t  figures_description:\n527\t  - Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds) — slower convergence than Walker\n528\t    Walk, higher post-convergence variance, asymptote ~700-750.\n529\t  analysis: 'Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization.\n530\t    Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained\n531\t    dt_emb experiments.\n532\t\n533\t    Lower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment\n534\t    comparison is valid.\n535\t\n536\t    '\n537\t- name: Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n538\t  plan_id: plan_021\n539\t  description: Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb\n540\t    triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\n541\t  expected_results:\n542\t    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n543\t      5K: 90\n544\t      15K: 215\n545\t      25K: 350\n546\t      35K: 460\n547\t      45K: 420\n548\t      55K: 340\n549\t      65K: 295\n550\t      75K: 270\n551\t      85K: 310\n552\t      95K: 330\n553\t      105K: 345\n554\t    seed0_nve_trajectory:\n555\t      25K: 0.84\n556\t      35K: 1.24\n557\t      45K: 1.85\n558\t      55K: 2.38\n559\t      65K: 2.12\n560\t      75K: 1.65\n561\t      85K: 1.3\n562\t      95K: 1.08\n563\t      105K: 0.95\n564\t    seed0_collapse: true\n565\t    seed0_collapse_onset: 45K\n566\t    seed0_nve_peak: 2.38\n567\t    seed0_nve_peak_step: 55K\n568\t    seed0_eval_nadir: 270\n569\t    seed0_partial_recovery: true\n570\t    seed0_eval_105K: 345\n571\t    seed0_vs_baseline: -52%\n572\t    seed1_eval_trajectory:\n573\t      25K: 360\n574\t      35K: 455\n575\t      45K: 510\n576\t      55K: 540\n577\t      65K: 520\n578\t      75K: 545\n579\t      85K: 555\n580\t      95K: 560\n581\t      105K: 568\n582\t    seed1_nve_trajectory:\n583\t      25K: 0.63\n584\t      35K: 0.95\n585\t      45K: 1.18\n586\t      55K: 1.38\n587\t      65K: 1.22\n588\t      75K: 1.08\n589\t      85K: 0.97\n590\t      95K: 0.92\n591\t      105K: 0.88\n592\t    seed1_collapse: false\n593\t    seed1_nve_peak: 1.38\n594\t    seed1_nve_peak_step: 55K\n595\t    seed1_eval_105K: 568\n596\t    seed1_vs_baseline: -21%\n597\t    seed2_eval_trajectory:\n598\t      25K: 340\n599\t      35K: 430\n600\t      45K: 470\n601\t      55K: 415\n602\t      65K: 440\n603\t      75K: 480\n604\t      85K: 490\n605\t      95K: 505\n606\t      105K: 510\n607\t    seed2_nve_trajectory:\n608\t      25K: 0.74\n609\t      35K: 1.1\n610\t      45K: 1.47\n611\t      55K: 1.35\n612\t      65K: 1.12\n613\t      75K: 0.93\n614\t      85K: 0.85\n615\t      95K: 0.8\n616\t      105K: 0.78\n617\t    seed2_collapse: false\n618\t    seed2_nve_peak: 1.47\n619\t    seed2_nve_peak_step: 45K\n620\t    seed2_eval_105K: 510\n621\t    seed2_vs_baseline: -29%\n622\t    collapse_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n623\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n624\t    mean_degradation: 34%\n625\t    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: 1/3\n626\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially:\n627\t    nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker''s 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker''s 1/2).\n628\t\n629\t    The weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run''s\n630\t    RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk''s. Cheetah dynamics (running\n631\t    gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM\n632\t    predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced\n633\t    value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs\n634\t    Walker''s 1.62-2.05x).\n635\t\n636\t    Seed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks\n637\t    2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers\n638\t    (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d''s terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah''s\n639\t    stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\n640\t\n641\t    Seeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x\n642\t    threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x\n643\t    threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3\n644\t    Cheetah trained seeds.\n645\t\n646\t    Mean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\n647\t\n648\t    '\n649\t  tables:\n650\t  - title: 'Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome'\n651\t    rows:\n652\t    - seed: 0\n653\t      nve_peak: 2.38\n654\t      nve_peak_step: 55K\n655\t      eval_105K: 345\n656\t      outcome: collapse + partial recovery\n657\t      nve_exceeds_1_5x: true\n658\t    - seed: 1\n659\t      nve_peak: 1.38\n660\t      nve_peak_step: 55K\n661\t      eval_105K: 568\n662\t      outcome: moderate degradation\n663\t      nve_exceeds_1_5x: false\n664\t    - seed: 2\n665\t      nve_peak: 1.47\n666\t      nve_peak_step: 45K\n667\t      eval_105K: 510\n668\t      outcome: moderate degradation + self-correction\n669\t      nve_exceeds_1_5x: false\n670\t  - title: Cross-environment CIVO comparison\n671\t    rows:\n672\t    - env: Walker Walk\n673\t      nve_exceeds_1_5x: 2/2 (100%)\n674\t      collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n675\t      mean_nve_peak: 2.07\n676\t    - env: Cheetah Run\n677\t      nve_exceeds_1_5x: 1/3 (33%)\n678\t      collapse_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n679\t      mean_nve_peak: 1.74\n680\t  figures_description:\n681\t  - 'Cheetah Run trained dt_emb 3-seed overlay: one collapses at 45K (partial recovery),\n682\t    two plateau. Annotate nve peaks.'\n683\t  - 'Cross-environment NVE comparison: Walker 2/2 above 1.5x threshold; Cheetah only\n684\t    1/3 above. Horizontal dashed line at 1.5x.'\n685\t  analysis: 'CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal\n686\t    pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3\n687\t    vs 2/2).\n688\t\n689\t    Two borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced\n690\t    intensity — consistent with Cheetah''s smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\n691\t\n692\t    Paper framing: \"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\" is weakened to \"triggers\n693\t    with high probability, environment-dependent severity\". The 1.5x threshold requires\n694\t    environment-specific calibration.\n695\t\n696\t    Combined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for\n697\t    diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\n698\t\n699\t    '\n700\t- name: Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n701\t  plan_id: plan_022\n702\t  description: 'Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO)\n703\t    in Cheetah.'\n704\t  expected_results:\n705\t    seed0_eval_105K: 510\n706\t    seed0_nve_peak: 0.78\n707\t    seed0_nve_105K: 0.55\n708\t    seed1_eval_105K: 480\n709\t    seed1_nve_peak: 0.85\n710\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n711\t    seed2_eval_105K: 530\n712\t    seed2_nve_peak: 0.72\n713\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.48\n714\t    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n715\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n716\t    mean_eval_105K: 507\n717\t    mean_vs_baseline: -29%\n718\t    reward_MAE_estimate: ~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\n719\t    reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2: ~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\n720\t  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally\n721\t    blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information\n722\t    → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\n723\t\n724\t    All 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating\n725\t    the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\n726\t\n727\t    Performance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction\n728\t    trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish\n729\t    dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning.\n730\t    This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\n731\t\n732\t    Cross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where\n733\t    seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530)\n734\t    because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\n735\t\n736\t    '\n737\t  tables:\n738\t  - title: Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\n739\t    rows:\n740\t    - env: Walker Walk\n741\t      seeds: 3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\n742\t      nve_range: 0.30-1.14\n743\t      collapse: 0/3\n744\t      eval_range: 148-503\n745\t    - env: Cheetah Run\n746\t      seeds: 3\n747\t      nve_range: 0.48-0.62\n748\t      collapse: 0/3\n749\t      eval_range: 480-530\n750\t  figures_description:\n751\t  - Cheetah dt_embed_dim=0 NVE trajectories (3 seeds) — all well below 1.5x. Less\n752\t    volatile than Walker 008f.\n753\t  analysis: 'Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments\n754\t    (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\n755\t\n756\t    Combined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction\n757\t    confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\n758\t\n759\t    '\n760\t- name: Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\n761\t  plan_id: plan_023\n762\t  description: Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\n763\t  expected_results:\n764\t    seed0_eval_105K: 575\n765\t    seed0_nve_105K: 0.65\n766\t    seed0_vs_baseline: -20%\n767\t    seed1_eval_105K: 535\n768\t    seed1_nve_105K: 0.72\n769\t    seed1_vs_baseline: -26%\n770\t    seed2_eval_105K: 550\n771\t    seed2_nve_105K: 0.78\n772\t    seed2_vs_baseline: -21%\n773\t    mean_eval_105K: 553\n774\t    mean_vs_baseline: -23%\n775\t    nve_range:\n776\t    - 0.55\n777\t    - 0.85\n778\t    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n779\t    zigzag_pattern: present in 2/3 seeds\n780\t    value_suppression_confirmed: true\n781\t  result_rationale: 'Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed\n782\t    to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation\n783\t    pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\n784\t\n785\t    Performance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker''s\n786\t    85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah''s higher observation complexity: untrained\n787\t    dt_emb adds more noise to a system that''s already harder to learn.\n788\t\n789\t    Zigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic\n790\t    actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real\n791\t    environment data.\n792\t\n793\t    Under median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE\n794\t    suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\n795\t\n796\t    '\n797\t  tables: []\n798\t  figures_description:\n799\t  - Cheetah untrained dt_emb eval trajectory — zigzag pattern similar to Walker 008a\n800\t    but lower asymptote.\n801\t  analysis: 'C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment.\n802\t    Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\n803\t\n804\t    Performance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34%\n805\t    degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\n806\t\n807\t    '\n808\t- name: 'RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions'\n809\t  plan_id: plan_030\n810\t  description: Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\n811\t  expected_results:\n812\t    cheetah_baseline:\n813\t      dt1_obs_mse: 15.8\n814\t      dt2_obs_mse: 28.5\n815\t      obs_gap: 1.8\n816\t      dt1_reward_mae: 0.012\n817\t      dt2_reward_mae: 0.017\n818\t      reward_gap: 1.42\n819\t    cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed:\n820\t      dt1_obs_mse: 38.5\n821\t      dt2_obs_mse: 48.2\n822\t      obs_gap: 1.25\n823\t      dt1_reward_mae: 0.065\n824\t      dt2_reward_mae: 0.168\n825\t      reward_gap: 2.58\n826\t    cheetah_no_dtEmb:\n827\t      dt1_obs_mse: 9.2\n828\t      dt2_obs_mse: 24.8\n829\t      obs_gap: 2.7\n830\t      dt1_reward_mae: 0.62\n831\t      dt2_reward_mae: 0.63\n832\t      reward_gap: 1.02\n833\t    key_finding: Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity\n834\t      gap → weaker CIVO\n835\t  result_rationale: 'Cheetah Run''s RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than\n836\t    Walker Walk''s (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah''s running gait\n837\t    has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker''s balance-and-walk task.\n838\t\n839\t    Smaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value\n840\t    overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically\n841\t    explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\n842\t\n843\t    The trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x)\n844\t    but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap\n845\t    (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\n846\t\n847\t    No dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f''s 1.01x) — architecturally,\n848\t    without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless\n849\t    of environment.\n850\t\n851\t    '\n852\t  tables:\n853\t  - title: Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\n854\t    rows:\n855\t    - env: Walker\n856\t      config: baseline\n857\t      obs_gap: 2.04\n858\t      reward_gap: 1.56\n859\t    - env: Walker\n860\t      config: trained_dtEmb\n861\t      obs_gap: 1.3\n862\t      reward_gap: 2.78\n863\t    - env: Walker\n864\t      config: no_dtEmb\n865\t      obs_gap: 2.75\n866\t      reward_gap: 1.01\n867\t    - env: Cheetah\n868\t      config: baseline\n869\t      obs_gap: 1.8\n870\t      reward_gap: 1.42\n871\t    - env: Cheetah\n872\t      config: trained_dtEmb\n873\t      obs_gap: 1.25\n874\t      reward_gap: 2.58\n875\t    - env: Cheetah\n876\t      config: no_dtEmb\n877\t      obs_gap: 2.7\n878\t      reward_gap: 1.02\n879\t  figures_description:\n880\t  - 'RSSM fidelity gap comparison: bar chart showing obs_gap for baseline models across\n881\t    Walker and Cheetah. Cheetah gap is smaller → explains weaker CIVO.'\n882\t  analysis: 'The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment\n883\t    comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\n884\t\n885\t    This provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics\n886\t    are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb\n887\t    exploits are smaller.\n888\t\n889\t    '\n890\t- name: Actor entropy analysis\n891\t  plan_id: plan_031\n892\t  description: Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\n893\t  expected_results:\n894\t    healthy_configs_entropy_pattern: gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5\n895\t      to -5.5\n896\t    civo_collapse_entropy_pattern: decrease accelerates before collapse → continues\n897\t      decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\n898\t    pathway_b_entropy_pattern: stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy\n899\t      destabilization)\n900\t    key_separation_metric: entropy change direction at collapse onset\n901\t    civo_examples:\n902\t      008d_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n903\t      009e_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n904\t      016e_entropy_95K: -3.62\n905\t    pathway_b_examples:\n906\t      008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n907\t      008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n908\t      delta: +1.38 (rising)\n909\t    healthy_examples:\n910\t      baseline_entropy_105K: -5.1\n911\t      008a_entropy_105K: -4.95\n912\t  result_rationale: 'Actor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs.\n913\t    Three distinct patterns emerge:\n914\t\n915\t    1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence.\n916\t    Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\n917\t\n918\t    2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic\n919\t    drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy\n920\t    stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\n921\t\n922\t    3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming\n923\t    more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\n924\t\n925\t    The entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing\n926\t    → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\n927\t\n928\t    Under median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2,\n929\t    possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\n930\t\n931\t    '\n932\t  tables:\n933\t  - title: 'Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B'\n934\t    rows:\n935\t    - experiment: 008d s0 (CIVO)\n936\t      onset_step: 35K\n937\t      entropy_before: -3.47\n938\t      entropy_after: -4.88\n939\t      direction: ↓ (over-commit)\n940\t    - experiment: 016e (CIVO)\n941\t      onset_step: 85K\n942\t      entropy_before: -3.62\n943\t      entropy_after: -3.62\n944\t      direction: ↓ then stable\n945\t    - experiment: 008e_v3_s2 (B)\n946\t      onset_step: 95K\n947\t      entropy_before: -5.21\n948\t      entropy_after: -3.83\n949\t      direction: ↑ (destabilize)\n950\t  figures_description:\n951\t  - 'Multi-panel entropy plots: (a) healthy configs (baseline, 008a, 008e_v3 s1),\n952\t    (b) CIVO collapse (008d, 016e), (c) Pathway B (008e_v3 s2). Annotate collapse\n953\t    onset with vertical line.'\n954\t  analysis: 'Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO\n955\t    from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\n956\t\n957\t    Under median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not\n958\t    sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion,\n959\t    not results.\n960\t\n961\t    '\n962\t- name: Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\n963\t  plan_id: plan_032\n964\t  description: Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\n965\t  expected_results:\n966\t    healthy_distribution: unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight\n967\t      spread\n968\t    civo_distribution: right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\n969\t    civo_pre_collapse_distribution: bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster\n970\t      of severely overestimated states\n971\t    untrained_distribution: unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider\n972\t      spread\n973\t    key_finding: bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse\n974\t      — potential early warning signal\n975\t  result_rationale: 'V(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to\n976\t    reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\n977\t\n978\t    In healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation\n979\t    (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3''s optimistic critic initialization.\n980\t\n981\t    In CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges —\n982\t    states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated.\n983\t    The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically\n984\t    inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the\n985\t    actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\n986\t\n987\t    In untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with\n988\t    wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise\n989\t    estimation in either direction.\n990\t\n991\t    This is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\n992\t\n993\t    '\n994\t  tables: []\n995\t  figures_description:\n996\t  - 'Histograms of V(s) - MC_return at 35K steps: (a) baseline (tight, centered),\n997\t    (b) 008d (right-shifted, heavy tail), (c) 008a (centered, wider spread).'\n998\t  analysis: 'The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic\n999\t    story: CIVO doesn''t uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1\n1000\t    imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\n1001\t\n1002\t    Under median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend\n1003\t    to Cheetah due to time constraints.\n1004\t\n1005\t    '\n1006\t- name: dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n1007\t  plan_id: plan_033\n1008\t  description: Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\n1009\t  expected_results:\n1010\t    initial_cosine_similarity: random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\n1011\t    trained_dtEmb_final_similarity: low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\n1012\t    trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity: divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings\n1013\t      maximally separated\n1014\t    untrained_dtEmb_similarity: constant at initial value (not trained)\n1015\t    frozen_dtEmb_similarity: constant at initial value (frozen)\n1016\t    key_finding: cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as\n1017\t      embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional\n1018\t      representations\n1019\t  result_rationale: 'dt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8).\n1020\t    As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the\n1021\t    model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\n1022\t\n1023\t    In collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because\n1024\t    the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional\n1025\t    differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories,\n1026\t    and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\n1027\t\n1028\t    In self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction\n1029\t    — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp\n1030\t    dt-distinction weakens.\n1031\t\n1032\t    This is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn''t essential for submission.\n1033\t\n1034\t    '\n1035\t  tables: []\n1036\t  figures_description:\n1037\t  - 'Cosine similarity between dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors over training steps.\n1038\t    Lines for: baseline (constant, no dt>1), 008d s0 (diverging, collapse), 009b s2\n1039\t    (diverging then partial convergence).'\n1040\t  analysis: 'Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides\n1041\t    an intuitive visualization of what \"trained dt_embedding\" means mechanistically.\n1042\t\n1043\t    '\n1044\t- name: NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\n1045\t  plan_id: plan_034\n1046\t  description: Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x,\n1047\t    2.0x across all seeds and environments.\n1048\t  expected_results:\n1049\t    walker_only:\n1050\t      threshold_1_3x:\n1051\t        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n1052\t        tp: 2\n1053\t        fp: 0\n1054\t        fn: 0\n1055\t        tn: 2\n1056\t      threshold_1_5x:\n1057\t        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n1058\t        tp: 2\n1059\t        fp: 0\n1060\t        fn: 0\n1061\t        tn: 2\n1062\t      threshold_1_7x:\n1063\t        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n1064\t        tp: 2\n1065\t        fp: 0\n1066\t        fn: 0\n1067\t        tn: 2\n1068\t      threshold_2_0x:\n1069\t        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n1070\t        tp: 2\n1071\t        fp: 0\n1072\t        fn: 0\n1073\t        tn: 2\n1074\t      auroc: 1.0\n1075\t    walker_plus_cheetah:\n1076\t      threshold_1_3x:\n1077\t        accuracy: 9/10 (90%)\n1078\t        tp: 3\n1079\t        fp: 0\n1080\t        fn: 2\n1081\t        tn: 5\n1082\t        note: Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\n1083\t      threshold_1_5x:\n1084\t        accuracy: 8/10 (80%)\n1085\t        tp: 2\n1086\t        fp: 0\n1087\t        fn: 3\n1088\t        tn: 5\n1089\t        note: Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\n1090\t      threshold_1_7x:\n1091\t        accuracy: 7/10 (70%)\n1092\t        tp: 1\n1093\t        fp: 0\n1094\t        fn: 4\n1095\t        tn: 5\n1096\t        note: only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\n1097\t      threshold_2_0x:\n1098\t        accuracy: 7/10 (70%)\n1099\t        tp: 1\n1100\t        fp: 0\n1101\t        fn: 4\n1102\t        tn: 5\n1103\t      auroc: 0.93\n1104\t    all_environments_including_cartpole:\n1105\t      threshold_1_3x:\n1106\t        accuracy: 13/16 (81%)\n1107\t        note: Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\n1108\t      threshold_1_5x:\n1109\t        accuracy: 11/16 (69%)\n1110\t        note: Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\n1111\t      threshold_1_7x:\n1112\t        accuracy: 10/16 (63%)\n1113\t        note: only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\n1114\t      threshold_2_0x:\n1115\t        accuracy: 10/16 (63%)\n1116\t        note: only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\n1117\t      auroc: 0.89\n1118\t    optimal_threshold: 1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\n1119\t    key_finding: 1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent\n1120\t      sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to\n1121\t      90% with no false positives.\n1122\t  result_rationale: '0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\n1123\t\n1124\t    Walker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks\n1125\t    0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained\n1126\t    nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\n1127\t\n1128\t    Cheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained\n1129\t    peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) →\n1130\t    false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed\n1131\t    1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained\n1132\t    > all untrained).\n1133\t\n1134\t    Cartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained\n1135\t    peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At\n1136\t    1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\n1137\t\n1138\t    Key insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment)\n1139\t    but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For\n1140\t    a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\n1141\t\n1142\t    '\n1143\t  tables:\n1144\t  - title: Threshold sensitivity matrix\n1145\t    rows:\n1146\t    - threshold: 1.3x\n1147\t      walker_acc: 100%\n1148\t      cheetah_acc: 100%\n1149\t      cartpole_acc: 83%\n1150\t      overall_acc: 81-90%\n1151\t    - threshold: 1.5x\n1152\t      walker_acc: 100%\n1153\t      cheetah_acc: 67%\n1154\t      cartpole_acc: 50%\n1155\t      overall_acc: 69-80%\n1156\t    - threshold: 1.7x\n1157\t      walker_acc: 100%\n1158\t      cheetah_acc: 67%\n1159\t      cartpole_acc: 50%\n1160\t      overall_acc: 63-70%\n1161\t    - threshold: 2.0x\n1162\t      walker_acc: 100%\n1163\t      cheetah_acc: 67%\n1164\t      cartpole_acc: 50%\n1165\t      overall_acc: 63-70%\n1166\t  figures_description:\n1167\t  - ROC curve for nve threshold across environments. Walker = perfect step function.\n1168\t    Cheetah = high AUC but threshold-sensitive. Cartpole = moderate AUC.\n1169\t  analysis: 'Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather\n1170\t    than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but\n1171\t    environment-specific calibration is needed.\n1172\t\n1173\t    Under median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold\n1174\t    recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\n1175\t\n1176\t    '\n1177\t- name: Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\n1178\t  plan_id: plan_050\n1179\t  description: dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\n1180\t  expected_results:\n1181\t    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n1182\t      5K: 45\n1183\t      15K: 220\n1184\t      25K: 420\n1185\t      35K: 580\n1186\t      45K: 670\n1187\t      55K: 730\n1188\t      65K: 770\n1189\t      75K: 800\n1190\t      85K: 820\n1191\t      95K: 830\n1192\t      105K: 835\n1193\t    seed0_eval_peak: 850\n1194\t    seed0_eval_final_10_mean: 838\n1195\t    seed1_eval_final_10_mean: 852\n1196\t    seed2_eval_final_10_mean: 827\n1197\t    iqm_final: 839\n1198\t    ve_ratio_stable: ~67-72%\n1199\t    ve_ratio_35K: 0.225\n1200\t    ve_ratio_45K: 0.32\n1201\t    ve_ratio_55K: 0.4\n1202\t    ve_ratio_105K: 0.67\n1203\t  result_rationale: 'Cartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3\n1204\t    achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF\n1205\t    pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\n1206\t\n1207\t    Faster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space\n1208\t    (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth),\n1209\t    (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\n1210\t\n1211\t    v/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the\n1212\t    actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\n1213\t\n1214\t    Under median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial.\n1215\t    The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler\n1216\t    environment.\n1217\t\n1218\t    '\n1219\t  tables: []\n1220\t  figures_description:\n1221\t  - 'Cartpole Swingup baseline: 3-seed learning curve with faster convergence than\n1222\t    locomotion tasks.'\n1223\t  analysis: 'Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference\n1224\t    from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\n1225\t\n1226\t    '\n1227\t- name: Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n1228\t  plan_id: plan_051\n1229\t  description: 'Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler\n1230\t    dynamics reduce CIVO?'\n1231\t  expected_results:\n1232\t    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n1233\t      25K: 390\n1234\t      35K: 530\n1235\t      45K: 610\n1236\t      55K: 660\n1237\t      65K: 690\n1238\t      75K: 700\n1239\t      85K: 710\n1240\t      95K: 710\n1241\t      105K: 712\n1242\t    seed0_nve_trajectory:\n1243\t      25K: 0.67\n1244\t      35K: 1.0\n1245\t      45K: 1.35\n1246\t      55K: 1.22\n1247\t      65K: 1.05\n1248\t      75K: 0.93\n1249\t      85K: 0.88\n1250\t      95K: 0.85\n1251\t      105K: 0.82\n1252\t    seed0_nve_peak: 1.35\n1253\t    seed0_collapse: false\n1254\t    seed0_eval_105K: 712\n1255\t    seed0_vs_baseline: -15%\n1256\t    seed1_nve_peak: 1.42\n1257\t    seed1_nve_peak_step: 45K\n1258\t    seed1_eval_105K: 685\n1259\t    seed1_vs_baseline: -18%\n1260\t    seed1_collapse: false\n1261\t    seed2_nve_peak: 1.22\n1262\t    seed2_nve_peak_step: 55K\n1263\t    seed2_eval_105K: 740\n1264\t    seed2_vs_baseline: -11%\n1265\t    seed2_collapse: false\n1266\t    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n1267\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n1268\t    mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n1269\t    mean_degradation: -15%\n1270\t    key_finding: CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold\n1271\t      in all seeds\n1272\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline).\n1273\t    The key mechanism is:\n1274\t\n1275\t    Cartpole''s RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\n1276\t\n1277\t    (a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\n1278\t\n1279\t    (b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\n1280\t\n1281\t    (c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\n1282\t\n1283\t    Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve\n1284\t    peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb\n1285\t    still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger\n1286\t    the CIVO feedback loop.\n1287\t\n1288\t    No collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where\n1289\t    the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the\n1290\t    dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and\n1291\t    non-catastrophic.\n1292\t\n1293\t    This is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve\n1294\t    > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn''t exceed 1.5x).\n1295\t    The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\n1296\t\n1297\t    '\n1298\t  tables:\n1299\t  - title: 'Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome'\n1300\t    rows:\n1301\t    - seed: 0\n1302\t      nve_peak: 1.35\n1303\t      nve_peak_step: 45K\n1304\t      eval_105K: 712\n1305\t      vs_baseline: -15%\n1306\t      exceeds_1_5x: false\n1307\t    - seed: 1\n1308\t      nve_peak: 1.42\n1309\t      nve_peak_step: 45K\n1310\t      eval_105K: 685\n1311\t      vs_baseline: -18%\n1312\t      exceeds_1_5x: false\n1313\t    - seed: 2\n1314\t      nve_peak: 1.22\n1315\t      nve_peak_step: 55K\n1316\t      eval_105K: 740\n1317\t      vs_baseline: -11%\n1318\t      exceeds_1_5x: false\n1319\t  - title: Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\n1320\t    rows:\n1321\t    - env: Walker Walk\n1322\t      mean_nve_peak: 2.07\n1323\t      nve_range: 1.62-4.12\n1324\t      exceeds_1_5x: 2/2 (100%)\n1325\t      collapse_rate: 1/2\n1326\t    - env: Cheetah Run\n1327\t      mean_nve_peak: 1.74\n1328\t      nve_range: 1.38-2.38\n1329\t      exceeds_1_5x: 1/3 (33%)\n1330\t      collapse_rate: 1/3\n1331\t    - env: Cartpole Swingup\n1332\t      mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n1333\t      nve_range: 1.22-1.42\n1334\t      exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n1335\t      collapse_rate: 0/3\n1336\t  figures_description:\n1337\t  - 'Cross-environment NVE peak distribution: Walker (high, all above 1.5x), Cheetah\n1338\t    (mixed), Cartpole (all below 1.5x). Shows monotonic relationship between environment\n1339\t    complexity and CIVO severity.'\n1340\t  analysis: 'Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which\n1341\t    correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\n1342\t\n1343\t    For the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion\n1344\t    tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism\n1345\t    has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\n1346\t\n1347\t    Under median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary\n1348\t    evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\n1349\t\n1350\t    '\n1351\t- name: Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n1352\t  plan_id: plan_052\n1353\t  description: Causal control for Cartpole.\n1354\t  expected_results:\n1355\t    seed0_eval_105K: 690\n1356\t    seed0_nve_peak: 0.82\n1357\t    seed1_eval_105K: 670\n1358\t    seed1_nve_peak: 0.75\n1359\t    seed2_eval_105K: 710\n1360\t    seed2_nve_peak: 0.8\n1361\t    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n1362\t    nve_exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n1363\t    mean_eval_105K: 690\n1364\t    mean_vs_baseline: -18%\n1365\t    reward_degradation: present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward\n1366\t      structure\n1367\t  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve\n1368\t    1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\n1369\t\n1370\t    Performance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning\n1371\t    → reward head can''t distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\n1372\t\n1373\t    The degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker\n1374\t    where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation.\n1375\t    In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop\n1376\t    doesn''t strongly differentiate them.\n1377\t\n1378\t    Under median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3\n1379\t    seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\n1380\t\n1381\t    '\n1382\t  tables: []\n1383\t  figures_description:\n1384\t  - 'Cartpole dt_embed_dim=0 NVE: all seeds below 1.0x, confirming causal control.'\n1385\t  analysis: 'Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds\n1386\t    exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\n1387\t\n1388\t    This is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding\n1389\t    in every environment tested.\n1390\t\n1391\t    '\n1392\t- name: Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\n1393\t  plan_id: plan_060\n1394\t  description: Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n1395\t  expected_results:\n1396\t    eval_105K: 580\n1397\t    nve_105K: 0.95\n1398\t    collapse: false\n1399\t    vs_baseline: -37%\n1400\t    truncation_frequency: ~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\n1401\t    effective_horizon: ~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\n1402\t    mechanism: truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value\n1403\t      estimates\n1404\t    ambiguity: delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\n1405\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous:\n1406\t    it delays but doesn''t clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\n1407\t\n1408\t    The mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction\n1409\t    becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates.\n1410\t    However:\n1411\t\n1412\t    (a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation\n1413\t    reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn''t prevent CIVO\n1414\t\n1415\t    (b) The RSSM''s own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during\n1416\t    the training period when CIVO develops\n1417\t\n1418\t    (c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but\n1419\t    not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\n1420\t\n1421\t    Expected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse,\n1422\t    but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation\n1423\t    but at the cost of learning efficiency.\n1424\t\n1425\t    Under median tier, this is reported as \"promising but inconclusive pilot\" in the\n1426\t    discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\n1427\t\n1428\t    '\n1429\t  tables: []\n1430\t  figures_description:\n1431\t  - 'Eval curve with truncation rate overlay: higher truncation correlates with lower\n1432\t    eval, showing the performance-safety trade-off.'\n1433\t  analysis: 'Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse\n1434\t    but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance\n1435\t    of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\n1436\t\n1437\t    Not included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\n1438\t\n1439\t    '\n1440\t- name: Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\n1441\t  plan_id: plan_040\n1442\t  description: Third DMC environment for generalization.\n1443\t  expected_results:\n1444\t    status_by_deadline: incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2\n1445\t      seeds) completed\n1446\t    baseline_eval_final: ~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\n1447\t    baseline_ve_stable: ~55-65%\n1448\t    trained_dtEmb_partial:\n1449\t      seeds_completed: 1\n1450\t      nve_peak: 1.45\n1451\t      collapse: false\n1452\t      eval_degradation: -25%\n1453\t    verdict: insufficient data to include in paper\n1454\t  result_rationale: 'P2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13\n1455\t    days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results\n1456\t    available.\n1457\t\n1458\t    Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah).\n1459\t    DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable:\n1460\t    the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for\n1461\t    NVE normalization.\n1462\t\n1463\t    The 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve\n1464\t    ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\n1465\t\n1466\t    Under median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line\n1467\t    mention in the limitations section.\n1468\t\n1469\t    '\n1470\t  tables: []\n1471\t  figures_description: []\n1472\t  analysis: 'Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken\n1473\t    rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\n1474\t\n1475\t    Recommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole\n1476\t    as supplementary).'\n1477\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40","uuid":"affc4c18-f5e8-409b-9c3d-d7fd7e0d0cb4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:52:20.295Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T115400/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Claims scoped to ''Walker Walk + Cheetah Run with consistent\n  mechanism''. Cartpole either dropped or presented as preliminary with borderline\n  CIVO signal (nve 1.2-1.5x, below diagnostic threshold).\n\n  NVE 1.5x threshold presented with environment-specific calibration caveat rather\n  than universal diagnostic. Cross-env accuracy ~80% (Walker 100%, Cheetah 67%, Cartpole\n  50% at 1.5x; improves to ~90%+ at 1.3x).\n\n  Dual-pathway finding (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B) mentioned in discussion\n  as ''evidence of deeper fragility'' but NOT elevated to core claim — Pathway B remains\n  under-characterized (2/4 seeds, mechanism unclear).\n\n  C2 strength adjusted: ''reliably triggers'' → ''triggers with high probability in\n  locomotion tasks''. Add explicit limitation on environment coverage.\n\n  TAWM contrast still holds (dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based\n  AC). Still submittable to CoRL as diagnostic paper; reviewers likely request revisions\n  on generality.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_009e_eval_105K: 131\n    seed1_009e_nve_peak: 4.12\n    seed1_009e_nve_peak_step: 55K\n    seed1_009e_collapse: true\n    seed1_009e_collapse_onset: 35K\n    seed2_009b_eval_105K: 268\n    seed2_009b_nve_peak: 2.05\n    seed2_009b_nve_peak_step: 25K\n    seed2_009b_collapse: false\n    seed2_009b_self_correction: true\n    seed2_009b_nve_105K: 0.38\n    collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 2/2 (100%)\n    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: 2/2\n  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\n\n    Trained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic\n    marker.\n\n    Collapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints\n    (feedback loop locks in); seed 2''s overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K),\n    allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\n\n    The bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay\n    buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm\n    corrective gradients.\n\n    Under median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized;\n    these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\n    rows:\n    - step: 25K\n      s1_eval: 167\n      s1_nve: 1.78\n      s2_eval: 141\n      s2_nve: 2.05\n    - step: 35K\n      s1_eval: 146\n      s1_nve: 1.71\n      s2_eval: 164\n      s2_nve: 1.24\n    - step: 45K\n      s1_eval: 141\n      s1_nve: 2.09\n      s2_eval: 141\n      s2_nve: 1.62\n    - step: 55K\n      s1_eval: 83\n      s1_nve: 4.12\n      s2_eval: 172\n      s2_nve: 1.41\n    - step: 65K\n      s1_eval: 250\n      s1_nve: 1.23\n      s2_eval: 209\n      s2_nve: 0.94\n    - step: 85K\n      s1_eval: 202\n      s1_nve: 1.15\n      s2_eval: 236\n      s2_nve: 0.51\n    - step: 105K\n      s1_eval: 131\n      s1_nve: 1.57\n      s2_eval: 268\n      s2_nve: 0.38\n  figures_description:\n  - Learning curves (eval return) for 008d seed 0 (original), 009e (seed 1), 009b\n    (seed 2) overlaid. Collapse at 55K for seeds 0 & 1; rising trajectory for seed\n    2.\n  - NVE trajectory showing 1.5x threshold line. Both seeds cross threshold; seed 2\n    crosses back down (self-correction), seed 1 sustains above.\n  analysis: 'Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold\n    = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\n\n    Combined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This\n    is the paper''s strongest quantitative result for C2.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_009f_eval_105K: 470\n    seed1_009f_nve_peak: 0.95\n    seed1_009f_collapse: false\n    seed1_009f_trend: zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\n    seed2_009c_eval_105K: 148\n    seed2_009c_nve_peak: 1.26\n    seed2_009c_nve_105K: 1.14\n    seed2_009c_collapse: false\n    seed2_009c_trend: peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but\n      persistent decline\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 0/2 (0%)\n    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained: 2/2\n    cross_seed_consistency: poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\n  result_rationale: '009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed;\n    re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\n\n    Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic\n    cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve\n    stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\n\n    The cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward\n    prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The\n    reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d''s\n    2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\n\n    C2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x\n    (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward\n    quality trade-off.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\n    rows:\n    - step: 25K\n      s0_nve: 1.02\n      s1_nve: 0.95\n      s2_nve: 1.26\n    - step: 45K\n      s0_nve: 0.83\n      s1_nve: 0.75\n      s2_nve: 0.72\n    - step: 65K\n      s0_nve: 0.37\n      s1_nve: 0.45\n      s2_nve: 0.53\n    - step: 85K\n      s0_nve: 0.65\n      s1_nve: 0.55\n      s2_nve: 0.6\n    - step: 105K\n      s0_nve: 0.3\n      s1_nve: 0.4\n      s2_nve: 1.14\n  figures_description:\n  - NVE trajectories for 008f (3 seeds) all below 1.5x threshold line. Contrast with\n    008d trained dt_emb (2/2 exceed 1.5x).\n  analysis: 'All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification\n    for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\n\n    009c''s late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent\n    with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\n\n    The practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal\n    control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample\n    OFF + H=15 config.\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 720\n    seed1_nve_range:\n    - 0.55\n    - 0.85\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed1_vs_baseline_105K: -21%\n    seed1_zigzag_pattern: true\n    seed2_eval_105K: 680\n    seed2_nve_range:\n    - 0.6\n    - 0.9\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.7\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    seed2_vs_baseline_105K: -26%\n    seed2_zigzag_pattern: true\n    mean_eval_105K: 726\n    mean_vs_baseline: -21%\n    nve_structural_suppression_confirmed: true\n  result_rationale: '008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with\n    two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the\n    structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\n\n    The zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never\n    exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still\n    introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by\n    self-correction from real environment data.\n\n    NVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature\n    of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens\n    over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline,\n    confirming it''s a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\n\n    Seed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0''s 778\n    (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align\n    favorably.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 008a multi-seed summary\n    rows:\n    - seed: 0\n      eval_105K: 778\n      nve_105K: 0.59\n      vs_baseline: -15%\n      zigzag_cycles: 2\n    - seed: 1\n      eval_105K: 720\n      nve_105K: 0.62\n      vs_baseline: -21%\n      zigzag_cycles: 2\n    - seed: 2\n      eval_105K: 680\n      nve_105K: 0.7\n      vs_baseline: -26%\n      zigzag_cycles: 1.5\n  figures_description:\n  - Three-seed overlay of 008a eval curves showing synchronous zigzag pattern. All\n    seeds show dip-recovery cycles with no terminal collapse.\n  - Three-seed NVE overlay showing consistent suppression below 1.0x from 55K onwards.\n  analysis: 'Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse).\n    C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\n\n    Performance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed\n    variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\n\n    Key contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed\n    1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests\n    whether CIVO is eliminated.\n  expected_results:\n    seed1_eval_105K: 870\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.8\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed1_pathway: healthy\n    seed2_eval_105K: 137\n    seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n    seed2_collapse: true\n    seed2_collapse_onset: 95K\n    seed2_pathway: Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\n    seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse: -3.83\n    seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse: -5.21\n    collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n    c6_verdict: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\n    gate_dt_1_frac: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\n\n    Seed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates\n    CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value\n    exploitation.\n\n    Seed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This\n    collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\n\n    — 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits\n    to wrong policy)\n\n    — 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\n\n    This is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions\n    in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The\n    dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\n\n    009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not\n    seed-fragile.\n\n    Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse\n    rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn''t reduce collapse\n    probability — it changes the pathway.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\n    rows:\n    - feature: value_change\n      pathway_A_008d_s1: +130% (60→138)\n      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: +13% (339→384)\n    - feature: eval_change\n      pathway_A_008d_s1: -43% (146→83)\n      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: -81% (715→137)\n    - feature: nve_driver\n      pathway_A_008d_s1: value numerator (explosion)\n      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: eval denominator (crash)\n    - feature: actor_entropy\n      pathway_A_008d_s1: ↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\n      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: ↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\n    - feature: mechanism\n      pathway_A_008d_s1: critic overestimate → actor exploit\n      pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side panels: (a) 008e_v3 seed 1 healthy learning curve vs seed 2 late-stage\n    collapse at 95K. (b) NVE trajectories diverge sharply at 95K. (c) Actor entropy:\n    seed 1 stable, seed 2 rises during collapse.'\n  analysis: 'C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection.\n    Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\n\n    Pathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different\n    from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\n\n    Under median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate\n    with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as\n    ''evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding''.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\n  plan_id: plan_015\n  description: 'D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init).\n    Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.'\n  expected_results:\n    eval_105K: 204\n    nve_105K: 0.42\n    nve_range:\n    - 0.39\n    - 0.47\n    collapse: false\n    collapse_mechanism: gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\n    actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n    actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n    gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n    vs_baseline_105K: -78%\n    verdict: NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\n  result_rationale: 'Anchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\n\n    Frozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic\n    cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding.\n    This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\n\n    However, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is\n    random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero\n    gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely\n    degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\n\n    This result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides\n    a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational\n    capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical\n    dt conditioning.\n\n    Contributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation}\n    × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each\n    intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - Eval trajectory showing high volatility (80-238) with no convergence pattern,\n    contrasted with 008d's directional collapse and 008a's zigzag recovery.\n  analysis: 'Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not\n    a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\n\n    Combined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but\n    trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb\n    on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\n\n    '\n- name: Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\n  plan_id: plan_016\n  description: Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\n  expected_results:\n    seed3_eval_105K: 830\n    seed3_nve_105K: 0.72\n    seed3_collapse: false\n    seed3_pathway: healthy\n    seed4_eval_105K: 195\n    seed4_nve_105K: 2.85\n    seed4_collapse: true\n    seed4_collapse_onset: 85K\n    seed4_pathway: Pathway_B (tentative)\n    seed4_actor_entropy_trend: rising during collapse\n    total_collapse_rate_4_seeds: 2/4 (50%)\n    pathway_B_characterization: under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent\n      across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized.\n    The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional\n    seeds collapses.\n\n    Seed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly\n    to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway\n    A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn''t fire.\n\n    Seed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops\n    first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due\n    to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\n\n    2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish\n    from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median\n    tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core\n    claim.\n\n    The under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but\n    cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model\n    representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift,\n    or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 008e_v3 all-seed summary\n    rows:\n    - seed: 1\n      eval_105K: 870\n      nve_105K: 0.8\n      collapsed: false\n      pathway: healthy\n    - seed: 2\n      eval_105K: 137\n      nve_105K: 3.78\n      collapsed: true\n      pathway: B\n    - seed: 3\n      eval_105K: 830\n      nve_105K: 0.72\n      collapsed: false\n      pathway: healthy\n    - seed: 4\n      eval_105K: 195\n      nve_105K: 2.85\n      collapsed: true\n      pathway: B\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Four-seed overlay: two healthy trajectories (seeds 1,3 converging ~830-870) and\n    two late-stage collapses (seeds 2,4 at 95K and 85K respectively). Shaded region\n    shows collapse zone.'\n  analysis: '2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination.\n    dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\n\n    Pathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern\n    is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\n\n    Paper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as\n    diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_020\n  description: dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion\n    environment.\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n      5K: 95\n      15K: 230\n      25K: 380\n      35K: 490\n      45K: 555\n      55K: 610\n      65K: 645\n      75K: 670\n      85K: 695\n      95K: 710\n      105K: 720\n    seed0_eval_peak: 755\n    seed0_eval_peak_step: 285K\n    seed0_eval_last10_mean: 738\n    seed1_eval_final: 720\n    seed1_eval_peak: 733\n    seed1_note: anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\n    seed2_eval_final: 695\n    seed2_eval_peak: 725\n    iqm_final: 718\n    iqm_last10: 722\n    ve_ratio_stable: ~65-70% from 135K+\n    ve_ratio_25K: 0.095\n    ve_ratio_35K: 0.21\n    ve_ratio_45K: 0.305\n    ve_ratio_55K: 0.385\n    ve_ratio_65K: 0.49\n    ve_ratio_75K: 0.58\n    ve_ratio_105K: 0.65\n  result_rationale: 'Cheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3\n    typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final\n    eval is [650, 800].\n\n    exp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current\n    657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence\n    than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\n\n    Cheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality\n    observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns\n    Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker\n    due to fast joint movements.\n\n    v/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic\n    architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker''s\n    69-72% due to Cheetah''s higher prediction difficulty.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\n    rows:\n    - seed: 0\n      eval_peak: 755\n      eval_final_10_mean: 738\n      ve_stable: 67%\n    - seed: 1\n      eval_peak: 733\n      eval_final_10_mean: 720\n      ve_stable: 65%\n    - seed: 2\n      eval_peak: 725\n      eval_final_10_mean: 695\n      ve_stable: 66%\n  figures_description:\n  - Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds) — slower convergence than Walker\n    Walk, higher post-convergence variance, asymptote ~700-750.\n  analysis: 'Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization.\n    Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained\n    dt_emb experiments.\n\n    Lower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment\n    comparison is valid.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_021\n  description: Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb\n    triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n      5K: 90\n      15K: 215\n      25K: 350\n      35K: 460\n      45K: 420\n      55K: 340\n      65K: 295\n      75K: 270\n      85K: 310\n      95K: 330\n      105K: 345\n    seed0_nve_trajectory:\n      25K: 0.84\n      35K: 1.24\n      45K: 1.85\n      55K: 2.38\n      65K: 2.12\n      75K: 1.65\n      85K: 1.3\n      95K: 1.08\n      105K: 0.95\n    seed0_collapse: true\n    seed0_collapse_onset: 45K\n    seed0_nve_peak: 2.38\n    seed0_nve_peak_step: 55K\n    seed0_eval_nadir: 270\n    seed0_partial_recovery: true\n    seed0_eval_105K: 345\n    seed0_vs_baseline: -52%\n    seed1_eval_trajectory:\n      25K: 360\n      35K: 455\n      45K: 510\n      55K: 540\n      65K: 520\n      75K: 545\n      85K: 555\n      95K: 560\n      105K: 568\n    seed1_nve_trajectory:\n      25K: 0.63\n      35K: 0.95\n      45K: 1.18\n      55K: 1.38\n      65K: 1.22\n      75K: 1.08\n      85K: 0.97\n      95K: 0.92\n      105K: 0.88\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed1_nve_peak: 1.38\n    seed1_nve_peak_step: 55K\n    seed1_eval_105K: 568\n    seed1_vs_baseline: -21%\n    seed2_eval_trajectory:\n      25K: 340\n      35K: 430\n      45K: 470\n      55K: 415\n      65K: 440\n      75K: 480\n      85K: 490\n      95K: 505\n      105K: 510\n    seed2_nve_trajectory:\n      25K: 0.74\n      35K: 1.1\n      45K: 1.47\n      55K: 1.35\n      65K: 1.12\n      75K: 0.93\n      85K: 0.85\n      95K: 0.8\n      105K: 0.78\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    seed2_nve_peak: 1.47\n    seed2_nve_peak_step: 45K\n    seed2_eval_105K: 510\n    seed2_vs_baseline: -29%\n    collapse_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n    mean_degradation: 34%\n    nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: 1/3\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially:\n    nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker''s 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker''s 1/2).\n\n    The weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run''s\n    RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk''s. Cheetah dynamics (running\n    gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM\n    predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced\n    value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs\n    Walker''s 1.62-2.05x).\n\n    Seed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks\n    2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers\n    (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d''s terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah''s\n    stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\n\n    Seeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x\n    threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x\n    threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3\n    Cheetah trained seeds.\n\n    Mean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome'\n    rows:\n    - seed: 0\n      nve_peak: 2.38\n      nve_peak_step: 55K\n      eval_105K: 345\n      outcome: collapse + partial recovery\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: true\n    - seed: 1\n      nve_peak: 1.38\n      nve_peak_step: 55K\n      eval_105K: 568\n      outcome: moderate degradation\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: false\n    - seed: 2\n      nve_peak: 1.47\n      nve_peak_step: 45K\n      eval_105K: 510\n      outcome: moderate degradation + self-correction\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: false\n  - title: Cross-environment CIVO comparison\n    rows:\n    - env: Walker Walk\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: 2/2 (100%)\n      collapse_rate: 1/2 (50%)\n      mean_nve_peak: 2.07\n    - env: Cheetah Run\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: 1/3 (33%)\n      collapse_rate: 1/3 (33%)\n      mean_nve_peak: 1.74\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cheetah Run trained dt_emb 3-seed overlay: one collapses at 45K (partial recovery),\n    two plateau. Annotate nve peaks.'\n  - 'Cross-environment NVE comparison: Walker 2/2 above 1.5x threshold; Cheetah only\n    1/3 above. Horizontal dashed line at 1.5x.'\n  analysis: 'CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal\n    pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3\n    vs 2/2).\n\n    Two borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced\n    intensity — consistent with Cheetah''s smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\n\n    Paper framing: \"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\" is weakened to \"triggers\n    with high probability, environment-dependent severity\". The 1.5x threshold requires\n    environment-specific calibration.\n\n    Combined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for\n    diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_022\n  description: 'Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO)\n    in Cheetah.'\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_105K: 510\n    seed0_nve_peak: 0.78\n    seed0_nve_105K: 0.55\n    seed1_eval_105K: 480\n    seed1_nve_peak: 0.85\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n    seed2_eval_105K: 530\n    seed2_nve_peak: 0.72\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.48\n    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n    mean_eval_105K: 507\n    mean_vs_baseline: -29%\n    reward_MAE_estimate: ~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\n    reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2: ~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\n  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally\n    blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information\n    → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\n\n    All 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating\n    the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\n\n    Performance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction\n    trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish\n    dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning.\n    This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\n\n    Cross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where\n    seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530)\n    because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\n    rows:\n    - env: Walker Walk\n      seeds: 3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\n      nve_range: 0.30-1.14\n      collapse: 0/3\n      eval_range: 148-503\n    - env: Cheetah Run\n      seeds: 3\n      nve_range: 0.48-0.62\n      collapse: 0/3\n      eval_range: 480-530\n  figures_description:\n  - Cheetah dt_embed_dim=0 NVE trajectories (3 seeds) — all well below 1.5x. Less\n    volatile than Walker 008f.\n  analysis: 'Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments\n    (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\n\n    Combined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction\n    confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\n\n    '\n- name: Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_023\n  description: Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_105K: 575\n    seed0_nve_105K: 0.65\n    seed0_vs_baseline: -20%\n    seed1_eval_105K: 535\n    seed1_nve_105K: 0.72\n    seed1_vs_baseline: -26%\n    seed2_eval_105K: 550\n    seed2_nve_105K: 0.78\n    seed2_vs_baseline: -21%\n    mean_eval_105K: 553\n    mean_vs_baseline: -23%\n    nve_range:\n    - 0.55\n    - 0.85\n    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n    zigzag_pattern: present in 2/3 seeds\n    value_suppression_confirmed: true\n  result_rationale: 'Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed\n    to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation\n    pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\n\n    Performance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker''s\n    85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah''s higher observation complexity: untrained\n    dt_emb adds more noise to a system that''s already harder to learn.\n\n    Zigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic\n    actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real\n    environment data.\n\n    Under median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE\n    suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - Cheetah untrained dt_emb eval trajectory — zigzag pattern similar to Walker 008a\n    but lower asymptote.\n  analysis: 'C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment.\n    Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\n\n    Performance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34%\n    degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\n\n    '\n- name: 'RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions'\n  plan_id: plan_030\n  description: Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\n  expected_results:\n    cheetah_baseline:\n      dt1_obs_mse: 15.8\n      dt2_obs_mse: 28.5\n      obs_gap: 1.8\n      dt1_reward_mae: 0.012\n      dt2_reward_mae: 0.017\n      reward_gap: 1.42\n    cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed:\n      dt1_obs_mse: 38.5\n      dt2_obs_mse: 48.2\n      obs_gap: 1.25\n      dt1_reward_mae: 0.065\n      dt2_reward_mae: 0.168\n      reward_gap: 2.58\n    cheetah_no_dtEmb:\n      dt1_obs_mse: 9.2\n      dt2_obs_mse: 24.8\n      obs_gap: 2.7\n      dt1_reward_mae: 0.62\n      dt2_reward_mae: 0.63\n      reward_gap: 1.02\n    key_finding: Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity\n      gap → weaker CIVO\n  result_rationale: 'Cheetah Run''s RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than\n    Walker Walk''s (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah''s running gait\n    has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker''s balance-and-walk task.\n\n    Smaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value\n    overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically\n    explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\n\n    The trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x)\n    but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap\n    (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\n\n    No dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f''s 1.01x) — architecturally,\n    without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless\n    of environment.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\n    rows:\n    - env: Walker\n      config: baseline\n      obs_gap: 2.04\n      reward_gap: 1.56\n    - env: Walker\n      config: trained_dtEmb\n      obs_gap: 1.3\n      reward_gap: 2.78\n    - env: Walker\n      config: no_dtEmb\n      obs_gap: 2.75\n      reward_gap: 1.01\n    - env: Cheetah\n      config: baseline\n      obs_gap: 1.8\n      reward_gap: 1.42\n    - env: Cheetah\n      config: trained_dtEmb\n      obs_gap: 1.25\n      reward_gap: 2.58\n    - env: Cheetah\n      config: no_dtEmb\n      obs_gap: 2.7\n      reward_gap: 1.02\n  figures_description:\n  - 'RSSM fidelity gap comparison: bar chart showing obs_gap for baseline models across\n    Walker and Cheetah. Cheetah gap is smaller → explains weaker CIVO.'\n  analysis: 'The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment\n    comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\n\n    This provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics\n    are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb\n    exploits are smaller.\n\n    '\n- name: Actor entropy analysis\n  plan_id: plan_031\n  description: Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\n  expected_results:\n    healthy_configs_entropy_pattern: gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5\n      to -5.5\n    civo_collapse_entropy_pattern: decrease accelerates before collapse → continues\n      decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\n    pathway_b_entropy_pattern: stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy\n      destabilization)\n    key_separation_metric: entropy change direction at collapse onset\n    civo_examples:\n      008d_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n      009e_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n      016e_entropy_95K: -3.62\n    pathway_b_examples:\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n      delta: +1.38 (rising)\n    healthy_examples:\n      baseline_entropy_105K: -5.1\n      008a_entropy_105K: -4.95\n  result_rationale: 'Actor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs.\n    Three distinct patterns emerge:\n\n    1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence.\n    Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\n\n    2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic\n    drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy\n    stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\n\n    3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming\n    more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\n\n    The entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing\n    → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\n\n    Under median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2,\n    possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B'\n    rows:\n    - experiment: 008d s0 (CIVO)\n      onset_step: 35K\n      entropy_before: -3.47\n      entropy_after: -4.88\n      direction: ↓ (over-commit)\n    - experiment: 016e (CIVO)\n      onset_step: 85K\n      entropy_before: -3.62\n      entropy_after: -3.62\n      direction: ↓ then stable\n    - experiment: 008e_v3_s2 (B)\n      onset_step: 95K\n      entropy_before: -5.21\n      entropy_after: -3.83\n      direction: ↑ (destabilize)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Multi-panel entropy plots: (a) healthy configs (baseline, 008a, 008e_v3 s1),\n    (b) CIVO collapse (008d, 016e), (c) Pathway B (008e_v3 s2). Annotate collapse\n    onset with vertical line.'\n  analysis: 'Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO\n    from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\n\n    Under median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not\n    sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion,\n    not results.\n\n    '\n- name: Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\n  plan_id: plan_032\n  description: Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\n  expected_results:\n    healthy_distribution: unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight\n      spread\n    civo_distribution: right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\n    civo_pre_collapse_distribution: bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster\n      of severely overestimated states\n    untrained_distribution: unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider\n      spread\n    key_finding: bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse\n      — potential early warning signal\n  result_rationale: 'V(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to\n    reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\n\n    In healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation\n    (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3''s optimistic critic initialization.\n\n    In CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges —\n    states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated.\n    The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically\n    inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the\n    actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\n\n    In untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with\n    wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise\n    estimation in either direction.\n\n    This is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Histograms of V(s) - MC_return at 35K steps: (a) baseline (tight, centered),\n    (b) 008d (right-shifted, heavy tail), (c) 008a (centered, wider spread).'\n  analysis: 'The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic\n    story: CIVO doesn''t uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1\n    imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\n\n    Under median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend\n    to Cheetah due to time constraints.\n\n    '\n- name: dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n  plan_id: plan_033\n  description: Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\n  expected_results:\n    initial_cosine_similarity: random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\n    trained_dtEmb_final_similarity: low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\n    trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity: divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings\n      maximally separated\n    untrained_dtEmb_similarity: constant at initial value (not trained)\n    frozen_dtEmb_similarity: constant at initial value (frozen)\n    key_finding: cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as\n      embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional\n      representations\n  result_rationale: 'dt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8).\n    As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the\n    model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\n\n    In collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because\n    the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional\n    differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories,\n    and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\n\n    In self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction\n    — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp\n    dt-distinction weakens.\n\n    This is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn''t essential for submission.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cosine similarity between dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors over training steps.\n    Lines for: baseline (constant, no dt>1), 008d s0 (diverging, collapse), 009b s2\n    (diverging then partial convergence).'\n  analysis: 'Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides\n    an intuitive visualization of what \"trained dt_embedding\" means mechanistically.\n\n    '\n- name: NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\n  plan_id: plan_034\n  description: Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x,\n    2.0x across all seeds and environments.\n  expected_results:\n    walker_only:\n      threshold_1_3x:\n        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n        tp: 2\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 0\n        tn: 2\n      threshold_1_5x:\n        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n        tp: 2\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 0\n        tn: 2\n      threshold_1_7x:\n        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n        tp: 2\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 0\n        tn: 2\n      threshold_2_0x:\n        accuracy: 4/4 (100%)\n        tp: 2\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 0\n        tn: 2\n      auroc: 1.0\n    walker_plus_cheetah:\n      threshold_1_3x:\n        accuracy: 9/10 (90%)\n        tp: 3\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 2\n        tn: 5\n        note: Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\n      threshold_1_5x:\n        accuracy: 8/10 (80%)\n        tp: 2\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 3\n        tn: 5\n        note: Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\n      threshold_1_7x:\n        accuracy: 7/10 (70%)\n        tp: 1\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 4\n        tn: 5\n        note: only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\n      threshold_2_0x:\n        accuracy: 7/10 (70%)\n        tp: 1\n        fp: 0\n        fn: 4\n        tn: 5\n      auroc: 0.93\n    all_environments_including_cartpole:\n      threshold_1_3x:\n        accuracy: 13/16 (81%)\n        note: Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\n      threshold_1_5x:\n        accuracy: 11/16 (69%)\n        note: Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\n      threshold_1_7x:\n        accuracy: 10/16 (63%)\n        note: only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\n      threshold_2_0x:\n        accuracy: 10/16 (63%)\n        note: only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\n      auroc: 0.89\n    optimal_threshold: 1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\n    key_finding: 1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent\n      sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to\n      90% with no false positives.\n  result_rationale: '0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\n\n    Walker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks\n    0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained\n    nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\n\n    Cheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained\n    peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) →\n    false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed\n    1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained\n    > all untrained).\n\n    Cartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained\n    peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At\n    1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\n\n    Key insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment)\n    but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For\n    a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: Threshold sensitivity matrix\n    rows:\n    - threshold: 1.3x\n      walker_acc: 100%\n      cheetah_acc: 100%\n      cartpole_acc: 83%\n      overall_acc: 81-90%\n    - threshold: 1.5x\n      walker_acc: 100%\n      cheetah_acc: 67%\n      cartpole_acc: 50%\n      overall_acc: 69-80%\n    - threshold: 1.7x\n      walker_acc: 100%\n      cheetah_acc: 67%\n      cartpole_acc: 50%\n      overall_acc: 63-70%\n    - threshold: 2.0x\n      walker_acc: 100%\n      cheetah_acc: 67%\n      cartpole_acc: 50%\n      overall_acc: 63-70%\n  figures_description:\n  - ROC curve for nve threshold across environments. Walker = perfect step function.\n    Cheetah = high AUC but threshold-sensitive. Cartpole = moderate AUC.\n  analysis: 'Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather\n    than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but\n    environment-specific calibration is needed.\n\n    Under median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold\n    recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_050\n  description: dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n      5K: 45\n      15K: 220\n      25K: 420\n      35K: 580\n      45K: 670\n      55K: 730\n      65K: 770\n      75K: 800\n      85K: 820\n      95K: 830\n      105K: 835\n    seed0_eval_peak: 850\n    seed0_eval_final_10_mean: 838\n    seed1_eval_final_10_mean: 852\n    seed2_eval_final_10_mean: 827\n    iqm_final: 839\n    ve_ratio_stable: ~67-72%\n    ve_ratio_35K: 0.225\n    ve_ratio_45K: 0.32\n    ve_ratio_55K: 0.4\n    ve_ratio_105K: 0.67\n  result_rationale: 'Cartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3\n    achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF\n    pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\n\n    Faster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space\n    (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth),\n    (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\n\n    v/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the\n    actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\n\n    Under median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial.\n    The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler\n    environment.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cartpole Swingup baseline: 3-seed learning curve with faster convergence than\n    locomotion tasks.'\n  analysis: 'Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference\n    from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_051\n  description: 'Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler\n    dynamics reduce CIVO?'\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_trajectory:\n      25K: 390\n      35K: 530\n      45K: 610\n      55K: 660\n      65K: 690\n      75K: 700\n      85K: 710\n      95K: 710\n      105K: 712\n    seed0_nve_trajectory:\n      25K: 0.67\n      35K: 1.0\n      45K: 1.35\n      55K: 1.22\n      65K: 1.05\n      75K: 0.93\n      85K: 0.88\n      95K: 0.85\n      105K: 0.82\n    seed0_nve_peak: 1.35\n    seed0_collapse: false\n    seed0_eval_105K: 712\n    seed0_vs_baseline: -15%\n    seed1_nve_peak: 1.42\n    seed1_nve_peak_step: 45K\n    seed1_eval_105K: 685\n    seed1_vs_baseline: -18%\n    seed1_collapse: false\n    seed2_nve_peak: 1.22\n    seed2_nve_peak_step: 55K\n    seed2_eval_105K: 740\n    seed2_vs_baseline: -11%\n    seed2_collapse: false\n    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n    mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n    mean_degradation: -15%\n    key_finding: CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold\n      in all seeds\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline).\n    The key mechanism is:\n\n    Cartpole''s RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\n\n    (a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\n\n    (b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\n\n    (c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\n\n    Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve\n    peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb\n    still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger\n    the CIVO feedback loop.\n\n    No collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where\n    the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the\n    dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and\n    non-catastrophic.\n\n    This is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve\n    > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn''t exceed 1.5x).\n    The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome'\n    rows:\n    - seed: 0\n      nve_peak: 1.35\n      nve_peak_step: 45K\n      eval_105K: 712\n      vs_baseline: -15%\n      exceeds_1_5x: false\n    - seed: 1\n      nve_peak: 1.42\n      nve_peak_step: 45K\n      eval_105K: 685\n      vs_baseline: -18%\n      exceeds_1_5x: false\n    - seed: 2\n      nve_peak: 1.22\n      nve_peak_step: 55K\n      eval_105K: 740\n      vs_baseline: -11%\n      exceeds_1_5x: false\n  - title: Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\n    rows:\n    - env: Walker Walk\n      mean_nve_peak: 2.07\n      nve_range: 1.62-4.12\n      exceeds_1_5x: 2/2 (100%)\n      collapse_rate: 1/2\n    - env: Cheetah Run\n      mean_nve_peak: 1.74\n      nve_range: 1.38-2.38\n      exceeds_1_5x: 1/3 (33%)\n      collapse_rate: 1/3\n    - env: Cartpole Swingup\n      mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n      nve_range: 1.22-1.42\n      exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n      collapse_rate: 0/3\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cross-environment NVE peak distribution: Walker (high, all above 1.5x), Cheetah\n    (mixed), Cartpole (all below 1.5x). Shows monotonic relationship between environment\n    complexity and CIVO severity.'\n  analysis: 'Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which\n    correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\n\n    For the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion\n    tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism\n    has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\n\n    Under median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary\n    evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\n\n    '\n- name: Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\n  plan_id: plan_052\n  description: Causal control for Cartpole.\n  expected_results:\n    seed0_eval_105K: 690\n    seed0_nve_peak: 0.82\n    seed1_eval_105K: 670\n    seed1_nve_peak: 0.75\n    seed2_eval_105K: 710\n    seed2_nve_peak: 0.8\n    collapse_rate: 0/3 (0%)\n    nve_exceeds_1_5x: 0/3 (0%)\n    mean_eval_105K: 690\n    mean_vs_baseline: -18%\n    reward_degradation: present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward\n      structure\n  result_rationale: 'Without dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve\n    1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\n\n    Performance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning\n    → reward head can''t distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\n\n    The degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker\n    where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation.\n    In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop\n    doesn''t strongly differentiate them.\n\n    Under median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3\n    seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cartpole dt_embed_dim=0 NVE: all seeds below 1.0x, confirming causal control.'\n  analysis: 'Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds\n    exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\n\n    This is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding\n    in every environment tested.\n\n    '\n- name: Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\n  plan_id: plan_060\n  description: Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n  expected_results:\n    eval_105K: 580\n    nve_105K: 0.95\n    collapse: false\n    vs_baseline: -37%\n    truncation_frequency: ~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\n    effective_horizon: ~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\n    mechanism: truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value\n      estimates\n    ambiguity: delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous:\n    it delays but doesn''t clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\n\n    The mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction\n    becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates.\n    However:\n\n    (a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation\n    reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn''t prevent CIVO\n\n    (b) The RSSM''s own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during\n    the training period when CIVO develops\n\n    (c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but\n    not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\n\n    Expected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse,\n    but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation\n    but at the cost of learning efficiency.\n\n    Under median tier, this is reported as \"promising but inconclusive pilot\" in the\n    discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Eval curve with truncation rate overlay: higher truncation correlates with lower\n    eval, showing the performance-safety trade-off.'\n  analysis: 'Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse\n    but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance\n    of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\n\n    Not included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\n\n    '\n- name: Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\n  plan_id: plan_040\n  description: Third DMC environment for generalization.\n  expected_results:\n    status_by_deadline: incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2\n      seeds) completed\n    baseline_eval_final: ~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\n    baseline_ve_stable: ~55-65%\n    trained_dtEmb_partial:\n      seeds_completed: 1\n      nve_peak: 1.45\n      collapse: false\n      eval_degradation: -25%\n    verdict: insufficient data to include in paper\n  result_rationale: 'P2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13\n    days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results\n    available.\n\n    Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah).\n    DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable:\n    the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for\n    NVE normalization.\n\n    The 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve\n    ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\n\n    Under median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line\n    mention in the limitations section.\n\n    '\n  tables: []\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken\n    rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\n\n    Recommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole\n    as supplementary).'\n","numLines":1477,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1477}}}
