{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kw5rmkV5BAyVNDHfhtoMUX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Temporal Abstraction.}\n5\tThe options framework \\citep{sutton1999options} and model-free action-repetition methods \\citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl, lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} achieve temporal abstraction by training values on real transitions, insulating them from model-induced bias. In world-model RL, the Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3} and transformer-based variants \\citep{micheli2023iris, robine2023twm, hafner2025dreamerv4} train policies in imagination at fixed $\\dt{=}1$. Several works introduce temporal abstraction via hierarchical representations \\citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros}, goal-conditioned jumps \\citep{li2025lsimagine}, or multi-scale state-space models \\citep{shaj2023mts3}, but none conditions a flat dynamics model on $\\dt$ for imagination-based policy training.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{TAWM and the MPC--Imagination Contrast.}\n8\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains. The critical distinction is architectural: TAWM re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into the critic. We show that identical $\\dt$-conditioning fails in DreamerV3's imagination loop, where errors compound through bootstrapping---transforming manageable imprecision into pathological value overestimation.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Value Overestimation.}\n11\tValue overestimation is well-studied in model-free \\citep{fujimoto2018td3, vanhasselt2016double} and model-based settings \\citep{janner2019mbpo, yu2020mopo}. \\citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints; our CIVO is mechanistically distinct, arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. \\citet{tallec2019making} show Q-function degradation with temporal resolution mismatches, underpinning our finding at $\\dt > 1$. \\citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts} demonstrate rollout-quality dominance in MBRL; we identify the specific $\\dt$-embedding-induced reward bias by which rollout divergence corrupts values. Our factorial diagnostic approach follows \\citet{engstrom2020implementation} and \\citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters}.\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013yKmM75ZfSV1WEq2K8kump","session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40","uuid":"bce5f6f8-a9ef-46ec-a034-c55affc28f7f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:53:50.743Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CDENohUorVQgDxXJsizxpQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01La2FcbfByTJqmZP1zJJuVh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work**, **framing/positioning**, and **significance**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis\n\nThe paper identifies CIVO (Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation) in DreamerV3 when using variable-rate (Δt>1) imagination with a trained Δt-embedding. A positive feedback loop develops: critic exploits Δt-conditional information → overestimates values for Δt>1 imagined states → actor exploits overestimated regions → corrupts replay buffer → reinforces overestimation. The paper introduces NVE (Normalized Value Excess) as a diagnostic metric and proposes NVE > 1.5× as a detection threshold.\n\n**Three core claims:**\n- C1: Trained Δt-embedding is necessary and sufficient for CIVO. Removing/freezing/not training eliminates it (0/11 seeds exceed NVE 1.5×). Training on Δt>1 data triggers it (Walker 2/2, Cheetah 1/3).\n- C2: CIVO severity scales with environment dynamics complexity (Walker > Cheetah > Cartpole, correlated with RSSM fidelity gap).\n- C3: Δt=1 imagination eliminates classic CIVO but not all collapse (2/4 seeds fail via distinct Pathway B with rising entropy).\n\n**Environments:** Walker Walk (primary), Cheetah Run (replication), Cartpole Swingup (boundary). All from DeepMind Control Suite.\n\n**Key Related Work cited:**\n- Temporal abstraction in RL: Options framework (Sutton et al. 1999), Precup 2000, FiGAR (Sharma et al. 2017), Skip-DQN (Dabney et al. 2021) — model-free, sidestep prediction quality issues\n- World-model RL: DreamerV1-V3 (Hafner et al. 2020, 2021, 2023), IRIS (Micheli et al. 2023), TWM (Robine et al. 2023) — share property that imagination quality bounds policy quality\n- Temporal abstraction in world models: TAWM (Nhu et al. 2025) — Δt-conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework, succeeds because MPC re-plans from real states\n- Value overestimation in MBRL: MBPO (Janner et al. 2019), delusional bias (Lu et al. 2018) — CIVO mechanism is distinct from policy-class constraints; it arises from learned conditioning amplifying model errors\n\n**Discussion/Framing highlights:**\n- CIVO mechanism: trained Δt-embedding → Δt-conditional reward exploitation → critic overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n- TAWM contrast: MPC re-plans from real states (bounds error compounding); DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic trains entirely in the model (allows self-reinforcement)\n- Pathway B: evidence of deeper fragility from trained Δt-embedding, under-characterized\n- NVE as practical diagnostic: AUROC ≥ 0.93 for locomotion, 0.89 across all envs\n- Recommends against naively introducing temporal conditioning in imagination-based world-model architectures\n\n**Limitations stated:**\n1. Only DreamerV3 with RSSM — generalization to transformer-based world models open\n2. Three DMC tasks only; Cartpole borderline; Hopper Hop incomplete\n3. Pathway B observed but mechanism not fully characterized\n4. NVE 1.5× threshold requires environment-specific calibration\n\n**References:** 14 total citations.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on novelty, significance, related work coverage, and framing. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (Is the core finding genuinely new? How does it advance beyond known value overestimation in MBRL? Is the CIVO mechanism sufficiently distinct from existing failure modes?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (Missing citations, under-discussed connections, misrepresented prior work)\n4. **Significance and Impact** (Who benefits? How actionable are the findings? Is this a niche DreamerV3 bug report or a broadly relevant insight?)\n5. **Framing / Positioning Critique** (Is the paper positioned correctly? Are claims calibrated to evidence? Over-selling or under-selling?)\n6. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n7. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, major vs minor)\n8. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n9. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous about novelty. Consider: (a) value overestimation in model-based RL is well-studied — what's genuinely new here? (b) the paper tests only DreamerV3 with 3 DMC tasks — how broad is the insight? (c) 14 references is thin for a top venue — what's missing? (d) Is \"diagnostic analysis\" enough contribution for a top venue, or does the community expect solutions?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14866,"cache_read_input_tokens":45891,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14866},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40","uuid":"864f688a-6bf7-4621-bddd-f3cf0fdabee8"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a9b4dc47210e8dc4c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01La2FcbfByTJqmZP1zJJuVh","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work**, **framing/positioning**, and **significance**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis\n\nThe paper identifies CIVO (Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation) in DreamerV3 when using variable-rate (Δt>1) imagination with a trained Δt-embedding. A positive feedback loop develops: critic exploits Δt-conditional information → overestimates values for Δt>1 imagined states → actor exploits overestimated regions → corrupts replay buffer → reinforces overestimation. The paper introduces NVE (Normalized Value Excess) as a diagnostic metric and proposes NVE > 1.5× as a detection threshold.\n\n**Three core claims:**\n- C1: Trained Δt-embedding is necessary and sufficient for CIVO. Removing/freezing/not training eliminates it (0/11 seeds exceed NVE 1.5×). Training on Δt>1 data triggers it (Walker 2/2, Cheetah 1/3).\n- C2: CIVO severity scales with environment dynamics complexity (Walker > Cheetah > Cartpole, correlated with RSSM fidelity gap).\n- C3: Δt=1 imagination eliminates classic CIVO but not all collapse (2/4 seeds fail via distinct Pathway B with rising entropy).\n\n**Environments:** Walker Walk (primary), Cheetah Run (replication), Cartpole Swingup (boundary). All from DeepMind Control Suite.\n\n**Key Related Work cited:**\n- Temporal abstraction in RL: Options framework (Sutton et al. 1999), Precup 2000, FiGAR (Sharma et al. 2017), Skip-DQN (Dabney et al. 2021) — model-free, sidestep prediction quality issues\n- World-model RL: DreamerV1-V3 (Hafner et al. 2020, 2021, 2023), IRIS (Micheli et al. 2023), TWM (Robine et al. 2023) — share property that imagination quality bounds policy quality\n- Temporal abstraction in world models: TAWM (Nhu et al. 2025) — Δt-conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework, succeeds because MPC re-plans from real states\n- Value overestimation in MBRL: MBPO (Janner et al. 2019), delusional bias (Lu et al. 2018) — CIVO mechanism is distinct from policy-class constraints; it arises from learned conditioning amplifying model errors\n\n**Discussion/Framing highlights:**\n- CIVO mechanism: trained Δt-embedding → Δt-conditional reward exploitation → critic overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n- TAWM contrast: MPC re-plans from real states (bounds error compounding); DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic trains entirely in the model (allows self-reinforcement)\n- Pathway B: evidence of deeper fragility from trained Δt-embedding, under-characterized\n- NVE as practical diagnostic: AUROC ≥ 0.93 for locomotion, 0.89 across all envs\n- Recommends against naively introducing temporal conditioning in imagination-based world-model architectures\n\n**Limitations stated:**\n1. Only DreamerV3 with RSSM — generalization to transformer-based world models open\n2. Three DMC tasks only; Cartpole borderline; Hopper Hop incomplete\n3. Pathway B observed but mechanism not fully characterized\n4. NVE 1.5× threshold requires environment-specific calibration\n\n**References:** 14 total citations.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on novelty, significance, related work coverage, and framing. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (Is the core finding genuinely new? How does it advance beyond known value overestimation in MBRL? Is the CIVO mechanism sufficiently distinct from existing failure modes?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (Missing citations, under-discussed connections, misrepresented prior work)\n4. **Significance and Impact** (Who benefits? How actionable are the findings? Is this a niche DreamerV3 bug report or a broadly relevant insight?)\n5. **Framing / Positioning Critique** (Is the paper positioned correctly? Are claims calibrated to evidence? Over-selling or under-selling?)\n6. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n7. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, major vs minor)\n8. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n9. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous about novelty. Consider: (a) value overestimation in model-based RL is well-studied — what's genuinely new here? (b) the paper tests only DreamerV3 with 3 DMC tasks — how broad is the insight? (c) 14 references is thin for a top venue — what's missing? (d) Is \"diagnostic analysis\" enough contribution for a top venue, or does the community expect solutions?\n","uuid":"3abc517b-afbe-40cd-b6f3-3601a302ff4a","session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work**, **framing/positioning**, and **significance**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis\n\nThe paper identifies CIVO (Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation) in DreamerV3 when using variable-rate (Δt>1) imagination with a trained Δt-embedding. A positive feedback loop develops: critic exploits Δt-conditional information → overestimates values for Δt>1 imagined states → actor exploits overestimated regions → corrupts replay buffer → reinforces overestimation. The paper introduces NVE (Normalized Value Excess) as a diagnostic metric and proposes NVE > 1.5× as a detection threshold.\n\n**Three core claims:**\n- C1: Trained Δt-embedding is necessary and sufficient for CIVO. Removing/freezing/not training eliminates it (0/11 seeds exceed NVE 1.5×). Training on Δt>1 data triggers it (Walker 2/2, Cheetah 1/3).\n- C2: CIVO severity scales with environment dynamics complexity (Walker > Cheetah > Cartpole, correlated with RSSM fidelity gap).\n- C3: Δt=1 imagination eliminates classic CIVO but not all collapse (2/4 seeds fail via distinct Pathway B with rising entropy).\n\n**Environments:** Walker Walk (primary), Cheetah Run (replication), Cartpole Swingup (boundary). All from DeepMind Control Suite.\n\n**Key Related Work cited:**\n- Temporal abstraction in RL: Options framework (Sutton et al. 1999), Precup 2000, FiGAR (Sharma et al. 2017), Skip-DQN (Dabney et al. 2021) — model-free, sidestep prediction quality issues\n- World-model RL: DreamerV1-V3 (Hafner et al. 2020, 2021, 2023), IRIS (Micheli et al. 2023), TWM (Robine et al. 2023) — share property that imagination quality bounds policy quality\n- Temporal abstraction in world models: TAWM (Nhu et al. 2025) — Δt-conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework, succeeds because MPC re-plans from real states\n- Value overestimation in MBRL: MBPO (Janner et al. 2019), delusional bias (Lu et al. 2018) — CIVO mechanism is distinct from policy-class constraints; it arises from learned conditioning amplifying model errors\n\n**Discussion/Framing highlights:**\n- CIVO mechanism: trained Δt-embedding → Δt-conditional reward exploitation → critic overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n- TAWM contrast: MPC re-plans from real states (bounds error compounding); DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic trains entirely in the model (allows self-reinforcement)\n- Pathway B: evidence of deeper fragility from trained Δt-embedding, under-characterized\n- NVE as practical diagnostic: AUROC ≥ 0.93 for locomotion, 0.89 across all envs\n- Recommends against naively introducing temporal conditioning in imagination-based world-model architectures\n\n**Limitations stated:**\n1. Only DreamerV3 with RSSM — generalization to transformer-based world models open\n2. Three DMC tasks only; Cartpole borderline; Hopper Hop incomplete\n3. Pathway B observed but mechanism not fully characterized\n4. NVE 1.5× threshold requires environment-specific calibration\n\n**References:** 14 total citations.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on novelty, significance, related work coverage, and framing. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty Assessment** (Is the core finding genuinely new? How does it advance beyond known value overestimation in MBRL? Is the CIVO mechanism sufficiently distinct from existing failure modes?)\n3. **Related Work Gaps** (Missing citations, under-discussed connections, misrepresented prior work)\n4. **Significance and Impact** (Who benefits? How actionable are the findings? Is this a niche DreamerV3 bug report or a broadly relevant insight?)\n5. **Framing / Positioning Critique** (Is the paper positioned correctly? Are claims calibrated to evidence? Over-selling or under-selling?)\n6. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n7. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, major vs minor)\n8. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n9. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous about novelty. Consider: (a) value overestimation in model-based RL is well-studied — what's genuinely new here? (b) the paper tests only DreamerV3 with 3 DMC tasks — how broad is the insight? (c) 14 references is thin for a top venue — what's missing? (d) Is \"diagnostic analysis\" enough contribution for a top venue, or does the community expect solutions?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01La2FcbfByTJqmZP1zJJuVh","session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40","uuid":"1386e47e-e2b8-4e2d-a223-fadb2cf25f52","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:53:54.354Z"}
