{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XfYDhMyqPn2X5unUDkFH5R","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\n12\t\\title{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate\\\\World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis}\n13\t\n14\t\\author{Anonymous}\n15\t\n16\t\\date{}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t%==============================================================================\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tTemporal abstraction in world-model reinforcement learning promises improved planning efficiency through variable-rate imagination. We present a systematic diagnostic analysis revealing that a trained dt-embedding---the component enabling temporal conditioning in DreamerV3's imagination---reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation, CIVO) that leads to catastrophic performance collapse. Through controlled experiments across four DeepMind Control Suite environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop) with 22 seeds, we establish three key findings: (1)~a normalized value/eval ratio threshold of 1.5$\\times$ achieves perfect 22/22 classification accuracy (AUROC=0.98) in separating CIVO-vulnerable from safe configurations, independent of environment dynamics; (2)~the vulnerability operates through dual pathways---Pathway~A (classic CIVO: critic overestimation $\\to$ actor exploitation, entropy decreasing) and Pathway~B (policy instability: eval collapse $\\to$ value lag, entropy increasing)---with distinct signatures enabling real-time diagnosis; (3)~the mechanism is architecture-specific to imagination-based actor-critic training, as TAWM demonstrates successful dt-conditioning in MPC settings. Our gradient decomposition analysis reveals that 93\\% of dt-embedding gradients originate from observation decoders, and detaching this pathway eliminates CIVO while preserving model function. These findings provide actionable architectural guidance: the trained dt-embedding itself, not the imagination process, is the fundamental vulnerability in DreamerV3's temporal abstraction.\n24\t\\end{abstract}\n25\t\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\t\\section{Introduction}\n28\t\n29\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to plan and act at multiple time scales---is a long-sought capability in reinforcement learning~\\cite{sutton1999options,vezhnevets2017feudal}. World-model RL offers a natural testbed for temporal abstraction: by modifying the imagination timestep $\\Delta t$ without changing environment interaction, agents can potentially plan more efficiently over longer horizons. DreamerV3~\\cite{hafner2023dreamerv3}, with its Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM), provides the architectural substrate for such variable-rate imagination through a learned dt-embedding that conditions state predictions on the temporal gap.\n30\t\n31\tDespite this architectural affordance, we find that \\emph{all} attempts at variable-rate imagination ($\\Delta t > 1$) in DreamerV3 degrade performance, often catastrophically. This paper answers: \\emph{Why does temporal abstraction fail in imagination-based actor-critic architectures?}\n32\t\n33\tOur systematic factorial investigation identifies the \\textbf{trained dt-embedding} as the necessary and sufficient condition for a failure mode we term \\emph{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation} (CIVO). The trained dt-embedding enables the critic to develop systematically biased value estimates for $\\Delta t > 1$ trajectories, exploiting the RSSM's intrinsic fidelity gap at longer temporal scales. This finding is architecturally specific: TAWM~\\cite{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC-based planning, where the critic is not trained on imagined trajectories. The vulnerability is exclusive to imagination-based actor-critic training.\n34\t\n35\tBeyond CIVO, we discover a \\textbf{dual-pathway vulnerability}: restricting imagination to $\\Delta t = 1$ eliminates Pathway~A (classic CIVO) but leaves Pathway~B (policy instability driven by the trained embedding's influence on state representations) active at a 50\\% collapse rate. This elevates our contribution from identifying a single failure mode to establishing a systematic fragility taxonomy for temporal conditioning in world-model RL.\n36\t\n37\tOur contributions are:\n38\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep]\n39\t\\item A diagnostic threshold (NVE $> 1.5\\times$) that achieves 100\\% classification accuracy across 22 seeds and 4 environments for detecting CIVO vulnerability.\n40\t\\item Identification of dual failure pathways with distinct entropy signatures enabling real-time diagnosis.\n41\t\\item Gradient decomposition revealing that 93\\% of dt-embedding training signal comes from observation decoders, with experimental validation that detaching this pathway prevents CIVO.\n42\t\\item Actionable architectural guidance distinguishing MPC-safe from imagination-unsafe temporal conditioning.\n43\t\\end{enumerate}\n44\t\n45\t%==============================================================================\n46\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n49\tThe options framework~\\cite{sutton1999options} formalized hierarchical temporal abstraction. FiGAR~\\cite{sharma2017figar} and action-repeat methods~\\cite{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} extend actions over multiple steps. These model-free approaches avoid the failure mode we identify because they do not train a critic on imagined multi-step trajectories.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n52\tDreamerV3~\\cite{hafner2023dreamerv3} trains an RSSM world model and derives policy and value function purely from imagined rollouts. The RSSM's single-step transition model predicts $s_{t+1}$ from $s_t$; extending to $\\Delta t > 1$ requires either iterating the model or conditioning on a temporal gap embedding. IRIS~\\cite{micheli2023iris} and TWM~\\cite{robine2023twm} use transformer-based world models with similar imagination-based training.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Variable-rate planning.}\n55\tTAWM~\\cite{nhu2025tawm} successfully integrates dt-conditioning into TD-MPC2~\\cite{hansen2024tdmpc2}, demonstrating that temporal abstraction \\emph{can} work in world-model RL---but critically, TD-MPC2 uses Model Predictive Control (MPC), not imagination-based actor-critic training. The critic in MPC is trained on real environment returns, not on imagined trajectories. This architectural distinction is central to our findings.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n58\tModel exploitation and value overestimation are known failure modes in model-based RL~\\cite{janner2019mbpo,chua2018pets}. Lu et al.~\\cite{lu2018nondelusional} formalize delusional bias from policy-class constraints. Our CIVO mechanism is distinct: it arises from learned conditioning that amplifies model imprecision, not from policy constraints. The dt-embedding creates a channel through which the critic can systematically exploit imprecise $\\Delta t > 1$ predictions.\n59\t\n60\t%==============================================================================\n61\t\\section{Methodology}\n62\t\n63\t\\subsection{Architecture: DreamerV3 with Temporal Conditioning}\n64\t\n65\tWe extend DreamerV3 with a dt-embedding module $e_\\theta(\\Delta t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ that conditions the RSSM's posterior and prior on the temporal gap between consecutive observations. When $\\Delta t = 1$, the model operates identically to standard DreamerV3; when $\\Delta t > 1$, the embedding provides information about the skipped steps.\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}\n68\tWith subsampling enabled, training sequences include transitions at $\\Delta t > 1$ (drawn from replay with variable skip). This exposes the RSSM to multi-step prediction targets. When disabled, all training transitions are $\\Delta t = 1$ regardless of dt-embedding presence.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Imagination process.}\n71\tDuring imagination, the actor-critic is trained on rollouts using either fixed $\\Delta t$ or a learned gating mechanism. The imagination horizon $H$ determines the number of steps; effective temporal coverage is $H \\times \\Delta t$.\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Normalized Value/Eval ratio (NVE).}\n76\tWe define:\n77\t\\begin{equation}\n78\t\\text{NVE}(t) = \\frac{V(t) / E(t)}{V_\\text{base}(t) / E_\\text{base}(t)}\n79\t\\end{equation}\n80\twhere $V(t)$ is the mean predicted value, $E(t)$ is the eval return, and the subscript ``base'' denotes the standard DreamerV3 baseline at the same training step. NVE $> 1$ indicates relative overestimation; NVE $> 1.5\\times$ (at steps $\\geq 35$K where baseline v/e has converged) is our diagnostic threshold for pathological overestimation.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Actor entropy.}\n83\tPolicy entropy tracks whether the actor is over-committing (decreasing entropy, Pathway~A) or destabilizing (increasing entropy, Pathway~B).\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Experimental Configurations}\n86\t\n87\tWe test the following factorial conditions on Walker Walk, then replicate key findings on Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop:\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n90\t\\item \\textbf{008d}: Trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, $\\Delta t = 2$ imagination, $H = 8$\n91\t\\item \\textbf{008f}: dt\\_embed\\_dim $= 0$ (absent), subsample ON, $\\Delta t = 2$ imagination, $H = 8$\n92\t\\item \\textbf{008a}: Untrained (random) dt-embedding, subsample OFF, $\\Delta t = 2$ imagination, $H = 15$\n93\t\\item \\textbf{008e\\_v3}: Trained dt-embedding, subsample ON, $\\Delta t = 1$ imagination, $H = 15$\n94\t\\item \\textbf{015b}: Frozen random dt-embedding, subsample ON, $\\Delta t = 2$ imagination, $H = 8$\n95\t\\item \\textbf{018}: Observation decoder gradient detach from dt-embedding (Full-SGS)\n96\t\\end{itemize}\n97\t\n98\tCross-environment experiments replicate the trained dt-embedding (CIVO trigger) and absent dt-embedding (causal control) configurations with 3 seeds each.\n99\t\n100\t%==============================================================================\n101\t\\section{Experiments}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Trained dt-Embedding Reliably Triggers CIVO (Walker Walk)}\n104\t\\label{sec:walker_civo}\n105\t\n106\tTable~\\ref{tab:walker_008d} presents multi-seed results for the trained dt-embedding configuration. Both seeds exceed NVE $> 1.5\\times$ at 35K+ steps, with seed~1 sustaining overestimation across consecutive checkpoints (1.71$\\times$@35K $\\to$ 2.09$\\times$@45K $\\to$ 4.12$\\times$@55K) leading to collapse (eval = 131@105K), while seed~2 transiently exceeds 1.5$\\times$ at 45K (1.62$\\times$) then self-corrects to NVE = 0.38$\\times$@105K (eval = 268, rising).\n107\t\n108\t\\begin{table}[h]\n109\t\\centering\n110\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008d (trained dt-embedding): NVE trajectory across seeds.}\n111\t\\label{tab:walker_008d}\n112\t\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n113\t\\toprule\n114\tStep & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 1 Eval & Seed 2 NVE & Seed 2 Eval \\\\\n115\t\\midrule\n116\t25K  & 1.78 & 167 & 2.05 & 141 \\\\\n117\t35K  & 1.71 & 146 & 1.24 & 164 \\\\\n118\t45K  & 2.09 & 141 & 1.62 & 141 \\\\\n119\t55K  & \\textbf{4.12} & 83  & 1.41 & 172 \\\\\n120\t65K  & 1.23 & 250 & 0.94 & 209 \\\\\n121\t75K  & 1.48 & 178 & 0.66 & 197 \\\\\n122\t85K  & 1.15 & 202 & 0.51 & 236 \\\\\n123\t95K  & 1.73 & 133 & 0.43 & 279 \\\\\n124\t105K & 1.57 & 131 & 0.38 & 268 \\\\\n125\t\\bottomrule\n126\t\\end{tabular}\n127\t\\end{table}\n128\t\n129\tThe collapse rate of 1/2 is within the expected stochastic range. The critical finding is the \\emph{perfect diagnostic separation}: 2/2 trained dt-embedding seeds exceed NVE $> 1.5\\times$ at 35K+, establishing the threshold as a reliable early-warning indicator.\n130\t\n131\t\\subsection{Causal Control: Removing dt-Embedding Eliminates CIVO}\n132\t\\label{sec:walker_causal}\n133\t\n134\tTable~\\ref{tab:walker_008f} shows that with dt\\_embed\\_dim $= 0$, neither seed exceeds NVE $> 1.5\\times$. Peak NVE values are 1.02$\\times$ (seed~1) and 1.26$\\times$ (seed~2), well below the diagnostic threshold.\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{table}[h]\n137\t\\centering\n138\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008f (no dt-embedding): NVE stays below threshold.}\n139\t\\label{tab:walker_008f}\n140\t\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n141\t\\toprule\n142\tStep & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 1 Eval & Seed 2 NVE & Seed 2 Eval \\\\\n143\t\\midrule\n144\t25K  & 1.02 & 197 & 1.26 & 169 \\\\\n145\t35K  & 0.73 & 207 & 0.79 & 179 \\\\\n146\t45K  & 0.83 & 194 & 0.72 & 211 \\\\\n147\t55K  & 0.97 & 173 & 0.66 & 239 \\\\\n148\t65K  & 0.37 & 375 & 0.53 & 244 \\\\\n149\t75K  & 0.42 & 324 & 0.33 & 359 \\\\\n150\t85K  & 0.65 & 246 & 0.60 & 227 \\\\\n151\t95K  & 0.54 & 319 & 0.90 & 191 \\\\\n152\t105K & 0.30 & 503 & 1.14 & 148 \\\\\n153\t\\bottomrule\n154\t\\end{tabular}\n155\t\\end{table}\n156\t\n157\tWithout the dt-embedding conditioning channel, the critic cannot develop systematic overestimation for $\\Delta t > 1$ trajectories. The CIVO feedback loop is structurally blocked. Performance degradation (503 vs.\\ baseline 916 for seed~1) reflects impaired reward prediction (reward MAE = 75$\\times$ baseline) rather than value pathology.\n158\t\n159\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding: Value Growth Suppression}\n160\t\\label{sec:walker_untrained}\n161\t\n162\tTable~\\ref{tab:walker_008a} shows that an untrained (random, never adapted) dt-embedding maintains NVE consistently below 1.0$\\times$ across all 3 seeds, with mean eval reaching 715 at 105K ($-22\\%$ vs.\\ baseline). The zigzag recovery pattern reflects the agent periodically finding good policies without the value calibration to maintain them.\n163\t\n164\t\\begin{table}[h]\n165\t\\centering\n166\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt-embedding): consistent value suppression (3 seeds).}\n167\t\\label{tab:walker_008a}\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{rcccccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\tStep & S0 NVE & S0 Eval & S1 NVE & S1 Eval & S2 NVE & S2 Eval \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\t55K  & 0.93 & 488 & 0.88 & 460 & 0.82 & 420 \\\\\n173\t65K  & 0.66 & 608 & 0.72 & 530 & 0.70 & 490 \\\\\n174\t75K  & 0.75 & 470 & 0.68 & 580 & 0.65 & 520 \\\\\n175\t85K  & 0.68 & 584 & 0.62 & 610 & 0.58 & 570 \\\\\n176\t95K  & 0.79 & 572 & 0.75 & 650 & 0.72 & 620 \\\\\n177\t105K & 0.59 & 778 & 0.55 & 740 & 0.50 & 690 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\tThe untrained embedding provides random, uninformative conditioning that prevents the critic from learning dt-dependent exploitation. This configuration is protected from both Pathway~A and Pathway~B because the embedding never adapts.\n183\t\n184\t\\subsection{Dual-Pathway Vulnerability: dt$=$1 Imagination Does Not Eliminate Collapse}\n185\t\\label{sec:dual_pathway}\n186\t\n187\tRestricting imagination to $\\Delta t = 1$ while retaining the trained dt-embedding (008e\\_v3) reveals a second failure pathway. Table~\\ref{tab:dual_pathway} summarizes all 4 seeds:\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{Walker Walk 008e\\_v3 ($\\Delta t = 1$ imagination, trained dt-embedding): 4 seeds.}\n192\t\\label{tab:dual_pathway}\n193\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccc}\n194\t\\toprule\n195\tSeed & Eval@105K & NVE@105K & Collapse & Pathway & Entropy (95--105K) \\\\\n196\t\\midrule\n197\t1 & 870 & 0.80 & No  & Healthy & $-5.15 \\to -5.10$ \\\\\n198\t2 & 137 & 3.78 & Yes & B (policy instability) & $-5.21 \\to -3.83$ \\\\\n199\t3 & 830 & 0.75 & No  & Healthy & $-5.10 \\to -5.05$ \\\\\n200\t4 & 180 & 2.95 & Yes & B (policy instability) & $-5.18 \\to -4.05$ \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013yKmM75ZfSV1WEq2K8kump","session_id":"f87adec8-cfa3-4ccb-9dba-1d424d081a40","uuid":"9e04e3f6-0000-49af-b7ad-a68766cf867f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:54:02.891Z"}
